        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Effects of Isostatic Uplift on Rivers:\n\nIsostatic uplift (land uplift due to post-glacial rebound or tectonic activity) produces two major effects on river systems:\n\n1. River rejuvenation: Uplift increases land elevation relative to sea level, creating new potential energy gradient. Rivers respond by renewed vertical erosion to reach new base level, creating rejuvenated river courses. The river develops increased velocity and erosive power, carving new valley sections and creating distinctive landforms including knickpoints (abrupt changes in gradient) and river terraces (remnants of former floodplain levels).\n\n2. Increased stream gradient and velocity: Isostatic uplift steepens the gradient between river source and sea level. Steeper gradient increases water velocity and erosive competence. Rivers erode downward more vigorously, transforming from mature meandering patterns toward youthful V-shaped valleys. Sediment transport capacity increases, enabling transport of larger material loads.",
        "diagram": "",
