        "label": "(iv)",
        "question": "Explain briefly one advantage of having improved transport infrastructure in Dublin.",
        "marks": 2,
        "model": "Improved transport infrastructure in Dublin reduces traffic congestion and cuts journey times.\n\nDublin's road network carries far more vehicles than it was designed for, and the resulting congestion costs the economy an estimated hundreds of millions of euro a year in lost working time and delayed deliveries. A high-capacity rail link such as Metrolink, running from Swords through the airport to the city centre, can move tens of thousands of passengers an hour on a segregated route that is unaffected by traffic. Each of those passengers is a car taken off the road, so journey times fall for everyone, including for the freight traffic serving Dublin Port.\n\nThe benefits follow from that: lower carbon emissions and better air quality, because public transport produces far less CO₂ per passenger-kilometre than private cars; better access to employment for people living in the outer suburbs and the commuter belt, which widens the labour market that Dublin employers can draw on; and a stronger case for firms to invest in the city, since reliable access to the airport is a location factor for the multinationals that dominate Irish exports. Land close to new stations also becomes viable for higher-density housing, which helps to address the housing shortage without extending urban sprawl.",
