        "label": "(iv)",
        "question": "Explain briefly one way that having a high percentage of people with a third level qualification can benefit an economy.",
        "marks": 2,
        "model": "A high percentage of third-level graduates attracts high-value foreign investment.\n\nModern multinational employers in technology, pharmaceuticals, medical devices and financial services choose where to locate largely on the quality of the workforce available, because the work they do requires engineers, scientists, software developers and accountants rather than unskilled labour. A country that can supply those graduates in quantity can attract firms that would otherwise go elsewhere. Ireland's own experience is the clearest illustration: the expansion of third-level education from the 1970s, much of it funded by EU structural funds, is a large part of the reason Intel, Pfizer, Google and Apple established European operations here, and multinationals now employ over 300,000 people directly in Ireland.\n\nGraduates also earn more, so they pay more tax and spend more in the domestic economy, and a well-educated workforce is more productive and more capable of adapting when technology changes — which makes the whole economy more resilient. Graduates are more likely to start businesses of their own, so an educated population generates indigenous enterprise as well as attracting external investment.",
