        "model": "Answer with reference to the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of Arklow issued with this paper.\n\nDraw the sketch with four ruled sides, roughly the same shape as the mapped area, and locate each feature by eye against the grid so that it is in approximately the right position and proportion. Label every feature on the sketch itself or in a key.\n\nWhat to show and label:\n• The coastline — drawn as a continuous line down the eastern side of the sketch where the land meets the Irish Sea, and labelled.\n• A beach — found from the sand symbol along the shore, outlined as a narrow shape against the coastline and labelled.\n• An area of mixed woodland — identified from the legend, which distinguishes mixed woodland from coniferous plantation, outlined as a closed shape and labelled.\n• The Avoca River — drawn as a single continuous line from where it enters the mapped area to its mouth at Arklow, and labelled.\n• An area of land over 200 m in height — traced from the 200 m contour, shaded, and labelled 'Land over 200 m'.\n\nMarks are awarded for the sketch outline and then for each of the five features separately, for showing it (graded on accuracy of location and proportion) and for labelling it. A feature that is shown but not labelled scores nothing for showing, and tracing the map rather than sketching it loses the outline marks.",
