    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "question": "(i) Name two materials released by a volcano during a volcanic eruption.\n(ii) What country, named in the text, had to evacuate at least 13,000 people because of volcanic eruptions?\n(iii) List two effects of volcanic activity that are mentioned in the report.\n(iv) Explain briefly one other positive effect of volcanic activity not mentioned in the report.\n(v) Name one example of a volcano that you have studied.\n(vi) Explain briefly what is meant by the term dormant volcano.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "(i) Lava / gas / ash / rock (ii) Indonesia",
        "diagram": "exam-images/geography/2020-OL-Q2-A.png",
        "source_page": 5,
        "scheme_points": 2,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "question": "Answer (i) or (ii).\n(i) Explain, with the aid of diagrams, the formation of any two landforms.\nOR\n(ii) Describe and explain any two processes of mass movement.",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "I will explain the formation of two landforms created by erosive processes. Gorge/Canyon Formation: A gorge or canyon forms through prolonged river erosion in areas of resistant rock, creating a steep-sided valley with vertical or near-vertical rock walls. Formation occurs when rivers flowing over hard rock (granite, limestone) create narrow channels through vertical erosion (downcutting). River velocity and sediment load enable cutting into bedrock, removing material grain by grain through abrasion and cavitation. Gorge formation requires millions of years of erosion in slowly-uplifting terrain maintaining river gradient and incision. Tectonic uplift in mountain regions drives rivers to cut downward, creating gorges like the Grand Canyon (Colorado River erosion in uplifting plateau) and Verdon Gorge (French Alps). The rock hardness and competence resist lateral erosion, directing erosion vertically and creating characteristic steep walls. Weathering of gorge walls through freeze-thaw on exposed rock creates talus slopes at the base. Example: The Grand Canyon, created by Colorado River erosion over 6 million years through resistant granite and sedimentary rock layers, with 1000+ meter depth demonstrating extreme vertical erosion. Waterfall Formation: Waterfalls form where rivers encounter sudden changes in rock resistance or elevation, creating vertical or near-vertical drops. Formation occurs at the junction between resistant cap rock and weaker underlying rock. Rivers eroding through layers of differing resistance create differential erosion: harder cap rock resists erosion while softer rock beneath erodes more rapidly, undercutting the cap rock. The unsupported cap rock eventually collapses, creating a vertical drop. Retreat of the waterfall occurs as the undermined cap rock progressively fails and the erosion cycle repeats upstream. Waterfalls ultimately disappear through continued erosion as the weaker rock erodes and the cap rock retreats upstream until gradient normalizes. Example: Niagara Falls formed at the boundary between hard dolomite cap rock and softer shale, currently retreating 0.6-1.0 meters per year through undercutting and collapse of the cap rock. Both gorge and waterfall formation demonstrate river erosion's power to create dramatic landforms through process-driven geomorphology over extended timescales.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 5,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "Describe and explain how humans interact with the rock cycle with reference to one of the following:\n• Quarrying\n• Mining\n• Oil/Gas exploitation\n• Geothermal energy production.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Humans interact extensively with the rock cycle through quarrying, which removes rock material from the Earth's crust for construction and industrial uses, disrupting natural rock formation processes. Quarrying operations extract stone materials including granite, limestone, and slate through open-pit mining methods, creating substantial landscape modifications. Quarrying removes rock material (endogenic rock formed through geological processes over millions of years) at rates far exceeding natural rock formation. Granite quarries in Scotland, Ireland, and globally remove igneous rock formed slowly through magma cooling underground, yet quarrying extracts this rock in decades rather than the geological timescales of formation. This creates mineral depletion and irreplaceable resource loss. Environmental impacts of quarrying include: landscape degradation through creation of large excavated pits and waste heaps; habitat destruction eliminating vegetation and animal populations; water pollution from quarry drainage and processing; air pollution from dust and blasting; visual impact on landscape character; and noise pollution from extraction activities. Quarrying removes vegetation and exposes parent rock to weathering, accelerating surface weathering processes as freshly exposed rock undergoes chemical and mechanical weathering. This altered weathering rate contrasts with natural weathering of gradually exposed rock. Positive aspects of quarrying include provision of essential construction materials enabling building and infrastructure development, and creation of secondary habitats when disused quarries are converted to wildlife reserves, wetlands, or recreational facilities. Quarried areas can be restored through infilling, vegetation planting, and ecosystem creation, partially reversing negative impacts. The rock cycle interaction demonstrates humans' significant modification of natural processes: extracting rock material much faster than natural regeneration, disrupting lithification processes, eliminating weathered sediment deposition in new sedimentary rock formation, and accelerating erosion-weathering cycles through exposed rock surfaces. Overall, quarrying demonstrates substantial human-rock cycle interaction, removing materials and disrupting processes at rates far exceeding natural regeneration, creating both resource benefits and significant environmental consequences.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 5,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
