    "id": "geography-pp-2020-OR-section3-populationmigration-Q10",
    "subject": "geography",
    "chapterId": "geo-population-migration",
    "sectionId": "geo-section3",
    "source": "LC Geography Ordinary 2020 Paper 2 — Q10",
    "year": 2020,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "question": "(i) Using graph paper, draw a suitable graph to illustrate this data.\n(ii) In your answer book, explain briefly one reason why the number of immigrants to Ireland has risen since 2010.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "(i) A suitable graph for this data is a line graph (trend graph), because the data shows how a single variable — the number of immigrants — changes continuously over time. A bar chart would also be accepted.\n\nHow to draw it on graph paper:\n• Title the graph 'Estimated Number of Immigrants to Ireland, 2010–2018'.\n• Put time on the horizontal axis, labelled 'Year', marked at even intervals: 2010, 2012, 2014, 2016, 2018.\n• Put the number of immigrants on the vertical axis, labelled 'Number of Immigrants', scaled from 0 to 90,000 with a division every 10,000. The scale must begin at zero and be evenly divided.\n• Plot the five points: 2010 = 42,000; 2012 = 57,000; 2014 = 67,000; 2016 = 82,000; 2018 = 90,000.\n• Join the points with straight ruled lines.\n\nMarks are awarded for the title, for labelling both axes, for an accurate and evenly divided scale, and for plotting each point correctly.\n\n(ii) One reason why the number of immigrants to Ireland has risen since 2010.\n\nThe Irish economy recovered strongly after 2012 and created far more jobs than the domestic workforce could fill. Multinational employers in technology, pharmaceuticals, medical devices and financial services expanded rapidly, and construction, health care and hospitality all needed staff. Unemployment fell from about 16% in 2012 to under 5% by 2018, so work was available.\n\nBecause Ireland is a member of the European Union, any EU citizen may come and take that work without a visa, and Ireland's employment permit system admits skilled workers from outside the EU as well. Ireland is English-speaking, politically stable and offers relatively high wages, which makes it an attractive destination. A large share of the arrivals are also returning Irish emigrants who had left during the recession and came back once jobs existed again.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 14,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "question": "Examine the aerial photograph of Arklow accompanying this paper. Explain three reasons why the town of Arklow developed at this location, using evidence from the aerial photograph to support each reason.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Arklow developed at its location through three key geographic advantages evident from the aerial photograph: First, coastal location providing maritime access and port facilities. The aerial photograph shows the town positioned directly on the coast at the mouth of the Avoca River. This coastal location enabled fishing, maritime trade, and communication with other coastal settlements. Historically, maritime trade was essential for commerce; coastal locations provided superior access compared to inland locations. The photograph shows harbor/port facilities enabling maritime trade. Second, river location at the Avoca River mouth providing freshwater access, water transport, and power sources. The photograph clearly shows the river flowing through the town. Rivers provided essential freshwater for drinking, agriculture, and industry. River transport enabled goods movement upstream to inland areas and downstream to ports. Water mills powered by river flow provided industrial power for grinding grain and other operations. The town's position at the river mouth enabled both river and coastal resource access. Third, level terrain visible in the aerial photograph permitted building and settlement development. The photograph shows the town area is relatively level and accessible, contrasting with surrounding hilly terrain. Level ground is essential for building construction, agriculture, and transport infrastructure. Steep terrain would require terracing and create development constraints. The combination of coastal access, river presence, and level terrain made Arklow an optimal location for settlement development in the region.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 14,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "(i) Name one city in the developing world that is growing rapidly.\n(ii) Explain one reason for the rapid growth of the city named by you in part (i) above.\n(iii) Describe and explain any three problems associated with the rapid growth of this developing world city.",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "(i) Bangalore (Bengaluru), India is a city in the developing world experiencing rapid growth. (ii) One reason for rapid growth: Bangalore's emergence as India's technology hub and information technology center attracted massive migration. Major multinational technology companies (Infosys, Tata Consultancy Services, IBM, Google, Microsoft) established large operations in Bangalore, creating hundreds of thousands of high-wage technology jobs. These jobs attracted educated young workers from across India seeking career advancement and higher incomes. Government policies supporting technology industry development and investment incentives accelerated growth. Climate and elevation (comfortable year-round weather) made Bangalore attractive. The technology boom transformed Bangalore from mid-sized colonial-era city to megacity of 10+ million people. (iii) Three problems from rapid growth: First, housing shortage and inadequate housing. Population growth far outpaced housing construction, creating severe shortages. Prices increased beyond affordability for lower-income migrants. Informal settlements and slums expanded accommodating workers unable to afford formal housing. Housing conditions in slums deteriorate with overcrowding, poor sanitation, and inadequate services. Second, traffic congestion and transportation crisis. Rapid population growth overwhelmed existing road infrastructure. Vehicle numbers increased exponentially without proportional road expansion. Traffic congestion creates 2-3 hour commutes for many workers, wasting time and reducing productivity. Air pollution from vehicle emissions degrades air quality and causes respiratory health problems. Public transportation remains inadequate, forcing reliance on personal vehicles and motorcycles. Third, water scarcity and environmental degradation. Rapid urbanization and population growth created enormous demand for freshwater that existing supplies cannot meet. Groundwater depletion from over-extraction reduced water table levels. Lakes and reservoirs serving the city became polluted and inadequate. Water supply interruptions and rationing occur during dry seasons. Inadequate wastewater treatment systems pollute water bodies and groundwater. Waste accumulation from garbage collection inadequacy creates public health hazards and environmental contamination. Industrial pollution from technology parks and manufacturing adds environmental pressure.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 14,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
