        "marks": 4,
        "model": "The statement is TRUE. A shield volcano is formed by lava flows of low viscosity. Low-viscosity (low-resistance) lava flows easily and spreads widely, creating the characteristic broad, gently-sloping shield volcano profile. The low viscosity prevents explosive eruptions, instead producing effusive eruptions with flowing lava. In contrast, high-viscosity lava creates steep-sided composite (stratovolcanoes) volcanoes with explosive eruptions. Shield volcanoes (Hawaii, Iceland) exemplify this relationship between lava viscosity and volcanic landform.",
