        "model": "Ireland's high ranking (top in EU) for STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics) graduates aged 20-29 provides substantial economic advantages. First, enhanced innovation capacity: STEM-trained workforce develops new technologies, patents, and research breakthroughs at higher rates than non-STEM populations. Ireland's tech sector (accounting for 7% of employment) generates EUR 180+ billion annually in exports and IP licensing; companies like Stripe (software), Intercom (SaaS), and numerous biotech firms emerged from Ireland's STEM talent pool. Second, competitive advantage in global labour markets: multinational technology companies (Google, Apple, Facebook, Microsoft) establish R&D centres in Ireland specifically because of abundant STEM talent, reducing recruitment costs 30-40% versus alternative locations like Silicon Valley where tech salaries exceed USD 200,000. This attracts high-wage employment and generates multiplier effects in supporting services. Third, economic resilience and productivity: STEM graduates earn 40-60% higher lifetime incomes than non-STEM graduates, generating higher tax revenue (approximately EUR 15,000 additional per worker over career); STEM skills adapt to economic changes, enabling workforce transitions as industries evolve. Automation and artificial intelligence adoption require STEM-trained workforce to implement effectively, so high STEM proportion positions Ireland to benefit from productivity gains while minimising displacement costs.",
