        "model": "(i) Two geographical locations where earthquakes have occurred: The San Francisco region (California, USA) - experiences frequent earthquakes due to the San Andreas Fault marking a conservative plate boundary where the Pacific and North American plates slide past each other. The Christchurch region (New Zealand) - experienced significant earthquakes including a devastating 2011 event (magnitude 6.3) near this city on the Alpine Fault. Other acceptable locations include: Tokyo, Japan (at destructive margin); Istanbul, Turkey (conservative boundary); Sumatra, Indonesia (subduction zone); or Nepal/Himalayas (collision boundary). (ii) Earthquake measurement scale: The Richter Scale - measures the amplitude of seismic waves recorded on seismographs, ranging from 1 to 9+, with each increase of 1 representing approximately 30 times more energy release. Alternative acceptable scale: the Moment Magnitude Scale (Mw), which is preferred by modern seismologists as it better represents energy release for larger earthquakes. (iii) How earthquakes occur and effects: Earthquakes occur when stress accumulated at plate boundaries exceeds the frictional strength of rocks, causing them to rupture suddenly and release energy as seismic waves. At destructive/convergent boundaries, oceanic crust subducts beneath continental crust, building stress that releases violently. At conservative/transform boundaries like the San Andreas Fault, plates slide laterally past each other, with friction causing 'stick-slip' motion. At constructive boundaries, plates separate and new crust forms. Seismic waves propagate outward as primary (P), secondary (S), and surface waves. Primary effects (direct from earthquake): ground shaking causes building collapse, landslides, liquefaction of soils, and rupturing of gas/water pipes. Secondary effects (consequences): fires from ruptured gas lines, tsunamis (from submarine earthquakes displacing water), landslides blocking valleys, and aftershocks causing further damage. Tertiary effects: economic disruption, displacement of populations, disease outbreaks, long-term reconstruction costs, and psychological trauma to survivors.",
