    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "(i) Name any one European Union policy that affects Ireland's economy; (ii) Describe and explain the effects of European Union policy(ies) on Ireland's economy.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "(i) The Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) is a European Union policy that affects Ireland's economy. Alternatively, the Single Market and Four Freedoms policy is another valid example.\n\n(ii) Effects of European Union Policy(ies) on Ireland's Economy:\n\nCommon Agricultural Policy Effects:\nThe CAP significantly impacts Irish agriculture, which employs approximately 7% of Ireland's workforce and contributes substantially to GDP. The CAP provides direct payments to farmers based on land area, supporting farm incomes and agricultural competitiveness. Price support mechanisms and market interventions help stabilise agricultural commodity prices. CAP funding supports rural development initiatives, environmental protection schemes, and agricultural infrastructure improvement. However, CAP regulations impose strict environmental and production standards that farmers must meet. The CAP has driven agricultural intensification in some regions with potential environmental consequences. Some critics argue the CAP supports large landholders disproportionately compared to small farmers.\n\nFour Freedoms Effects:\nEU membership grants Ireland access to the Single Market through the four freedoms: free movement of goods, services, capital, and labour. Free movement of goods eliminates tariffs and customs barriers, reducing costs for Irish exporters and allowing Irish businesses to access the larger EU market. This has benefited Irish food and beverage exports, pharmaceuticals, and manufacturing sectors. Free movement of services enables Irish financial institutions, professional services, and consultancy firms to operate throughout the EU. Free movement of capital allows Irish businesses and investors to access EU capital markets at competitive rates. Free movement of labour has enabled Irish workers to seek employment throughout the EU while attracting skilled workers to Ireland, supporting economic growth and innovation.\n\nTrade and Economic Growth:\nEU membership through these policies has stimulated significant foreign direct investment (FDI) into Ireland from multinational corporations seeking EU market access. Technology companies such as Google, Apple, Microsoft, and Intel have established major operations in Ireland. This FDI has created high-value employment, generated tax revenue, and supported the development of Ireland as a knowledge economy. EU trade represents approximately 55% of Ireland's total trade, demonstrating the fundamental importance of EU market access to Irish economic prosperity.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 12,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
