        "marks": 40,
        "model": "Birth rates and death rates influenced by numerous interconnected factors: Income levels (economic): High-income countries show low birth rates because: (a) education costly/delayed, reducing childbearing years; (b) childcare expensive, limiting family size; (c) women extensively in workforce, prioritizing career; (d) retirement security reduces need for child 'old-age insurance'; (e) social norms favor smaller families. Low-income countries show high birth rates because: (a) children provide economic labor on farms; (b) no pension systems, rely on children for elder support; (c) high infant mortality drives more children ensuring survival; (d) child costs lower (productive from young age); (e) education access limited, early childbearing common. Death rates correlate with income: high-income = low death rates (healthcare access, nutrition, sanitation); low-income = high death rates (disease, malnutrition, poor medical care). Culture/society (social factors): Religious traditions influence family size - Catholic/Islamic teachings traditionally support larger families; contraceptive use varies by religious affiliation/cultural tradition. Gender roles - women's education/employment restrictions tend toward higher birth rates. Status of motherhood - some societies attach high status to large families; others signal modernity with smaller families. Marriage customs - early marriage increases births; delayed marriage reduces births. Government policies: China's one-child policy reduced birth rates; family planning services reduce births; childcare subsidies (France, Sweden) increase births; pro-natalist policies in aging societies attempt higher births. Medical technology (healthcare): Contraceptive availability reduces birth rates directly - family planning services enable pregnancy spacing; reproductive health technology shifts birth rates downward. Maternal healthcare quality affects death rates - prenatal care, skilled birth attendants, emergency obstetric services reduce maternal mortality. Vaccination/disease prevention reduce infant/child mortality, paradoxically increasing population growth as fewer children needed. Healthcare system quality determines death rate - life expectancy increases with disease treatment access; healthcare access correlates with wealth. Climate/natural disasters: Food security affects both rates - famine increases death rates, may suppress births; water scarcity constrains health; disease prevalence influenced by climate (malaria, dengue in tropics elevates death rates). Sanitation/water quality: Access to clean water/sanitation dramatically reduces death rates from diarrheal disease (major killer in developing nations); inadequate sanitation increases infant/child mortality; these factors explain regional death rate disparities.",
