        "model": "(i) City where urban sprawl occurred: Las Vegas, Nevada, USA - exemplifies rapid sprawl consuming desert land. Alternatives: Phoenix (Arizona), Dubai (UAE), Los Angeles (California), Melbourne (Australia), Delhi (India). Las Vegas expanded 1990s onward; metro ~1.4 million (2000) to 2.3 million+ (2020). (ii) Reasons for urban sprawl: (a) Economic - cheap periphery land; developers found profitable undeveloped land; gambling/tourism drove employment; construction industry thrived; property speculation. (b) Cultural - American single-family home/yard preference; car-dependent culture making periphery commuting acceptable; escape city center congestion. (c) Government policy - low-density zoning; tax incentives for periphery development; inadequate expansion restrictions; infrastructure investment (roads, water, utilities) enabling sprawl. (d) Physical - surrounding desert 'empty' appearing available; Colorado River water enabled growth; flat terrain eased development. (e) Infrastructure - highway development (I-15, Beltway) made commuting feasible; dispersed employment centers (casinos, warehouses) created nodal sprawl rather than single downtown focus. (iii) Sprawl solution: Urban growth boundary (UGB) - establish defined development limit; restrict periphery development through planning; requires public land acquisition for protection. Alternatives: (a) Zoning - enforce minimum density; establish mixed-use walkable neighborhoods; restrict agricultural land conversion. (b) Transportation - develop efficient public transit (light rail, BRT) making urban residence viable; reduce car reliance; prioritize transit-oriented development. (c) Greenfield protection - purchase/protect agricultural/natural land; establish regional parks/natural corridors. (d) Urban renewal/intensification - redevelop existing areas; encourage infill (vacant/underutilized urban land); increase density; adaptive reuse of historic buildings. (e) Regulatory enforcement - strengthen planning laws; require environmental impact assessments; enforce building codes/density; charge developers for infrastructure costs.",
