        "label": "B",
        "question": "(i) State two reasons why people migrate from rural areas to urban areas; (ii) Describe and explain the effect of this migration on the urban areas that people migrate to; (iii) Describe and explain the effect of this migration on the rural areas they leave behind.",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "(i) Two reasons for rural-to-urban migration: (a) Economic opportunity - urban areas concentrate diverse employment (manufacturing, services, commerce, technology); higher wages than rural; diverse jobs offer better career prospects; lower urban unemployment; abundant informal employment; easier credit/capital access. (b) Education/skills - urban areas concentrate secondary/tertiary institutions; training course access; skills acquisition matching modern economy; often superior quality; specialized education (technical colleges, universities) unavailable rurally. Alternatives: healthcare (hospitals/specialists concentrated); improved living standards (electricity/water/sanitation better); social opportunities (entertainment, culture, networks); mechanization reducing rural employment; agricultural debt escape; family reunification (following migrants); political stability/safety concerns. (ii) Effects on urban areas: Positive - increased labor supply for expanding industries; larger consumer market supporting business; cultural diversity/innovation; increased tax base funding services; cultural vitality. Negative - housing shortage (demand exceeds supply, prices rise); urban sprawl consuming land; slum/informal settlement formation where housing inadequate; congestion/traffic pressure; water/sanitation/electricity infrastructure inadequate for growth; pollution/environmental degradation from density/industry; unemployment/underemployment if jobs unmatch migrant skills; social tensions between migrants/established populations; crime/social disorder where poverty concentrates; overcrowding in schools/healthcare; inadequate waste management. Urban areas attract continued migration despite challenges because conditions still offer better opportunity than rural alternatives. (iii) Rural area effects: (a) Depopulation - population decline as younger adults leave; reduced labor force for agriculture/rural industries; villages/towns shrink; business closure as consumer base diminishes; school closures; reduced services (shops, healthcare, transport) becoming uneconomical. (b) Economic decline - agricultural productivity falls from labor scarcity; traditional rural industries suffer; reduced economic activity/tax base; increased poverty; unemployment of those unable to migrate. (c) Demographic imbalance - elderly remain while young migrate, creating skewed age structure; aging population requires healthcare/social support; labor-intensive agriculture becomes difficult; few young adults for elderly care. (d) Social problems - reduced community cohesion; cultural tradition/rural identity loss; mental health issues among isolated elderly; youth without opportunities remaining; family separation/social stress. (e) Environmental - agricultural land abandonment (ecological recovery possible); but also landscape/heritage site neglect. (f) Infrastructure deterioration - maintenance expensive for smaller populations; services withdrawn (bus routes, post offices); housing stock deteriorates as occupied properties decline. Examples: Rural Ireland experienced significant out-migration to Dublin/cities; villages in West/Southwest experienced loss 1980s-2000s; agricultural communities contracted as farming mechanized/young left; similar patterns in Spain, Portugal, Poland.",
        "diagram": "",
