        "model": "Answer with reference to the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of Clonmel issued with this paper.\n\nThe sketch is drawn to half the scale of the mapped area, so the outline should measure 12 cm × 9 cm (a difference of up to 0.5 cm is allowed), with four ruled sides — using the edge of the page is not accepted. Draw the outline first, then locate each feature by eye against the grid and show it in approximately the correct position and proportion. Label every feature on the sketch or in a key.\n\nWhat to show and label:\n• The entire course of the River Suir — drawn as one continuous line from where it enters the mapped area to where it leaves it, and labelled. It must be the whole course, not a section.\n• An area of land over 400 m — traced from the 400 m contour on the Comeragh or Knockmealdown uplands, shaded as a closed area, and labelled 'Land over 400 m'.\n• The Waterford Cycling Trails cycle route from S 148 180 to S 200 218 — located from the cycle-route symbol in the legend, drawn between those two grid references only, and labelled.\n• An area of coniferous plantation north of Northing 24 and west of Easting 17 — identified from the coniferous woodland symbol, outlined as a closed shape lying inside both of those limits, and labelled. Choosing a plantation outside the stated area loses the showing marks.\n\nMarks: 4 for the sketch outline, then 4 for each of the four features — 3 for showing it (graded on accuracy) and 1 for labelling it. A feature shown but not labelled scores nothing for showing; a feature located incorrectly but named correctly still earns the labelling mark. An obvious tracing loses the outline marks.",
