        "question": "Explain briefly why the ocean crust found at E is older than the ocean crust found at D.",
        "marks": 2,
        "model": "The ocean crust found at E is older than at D because of seafloor spreading at mid-ocean ridges. At a constructive plate margin (Mid-Atlantic Ridge), new oceanic crust forms continuously as magma wells up from the mantle, creating new basalt rock at the ridge axis. This new crust is approximately 0-5 million years old at the ridge centre. As the tectonic plates move apart (currently 2.5-5 cm/year in the Atlantic), older crust is carried away from the spreading centre, pushing further from the ridge. Location D, closer to Iceland and the ridge axis, represents crust formed more recently (23-66 million years old as stated in the marking scheme), while location E, farther from the ridge axis, represents crust that formed earlier and has since moved away from the spreading centre. The age progression (younger at ridge axis, increasingly older with distance) reflects the chronological pattern of seafloor generation at constructive margins.",
