        "label": "A",
        "question": "Draw an outline map of Ireland. On it, show and name each of the following: The outline of one named region, The outline of a named feature of relief (upland or lowland) within that region, One named urban centre in that region, One named river in that region.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Answer with reference to the Western Region of Ireland (Counties Galway, Mayo and Roscommon).\n\nDraw a freehand outline map of Ireland about a quarter of a page in size, with the Atlantic to the west and the Irish Sea to the east. Then show and name each of the four required items, each earning marks separately for showing and for naming:\n\n• The outline of one named region — the Western Region, outlined as a closed shape covering Galway, Mayo and Roscommon, and labelled.\n• The outline of a named feature of relief within that region — the Twelve Bens, outlined as a closed shape in north-west Connemara and labelled. (The Central Lowlands around Roscommon would serve equally as a named lowland.)\n• One named urban centre in that region — Galway city, marked with a dot on the north shore of Galway Bay and labelled. (Castlebar, Westport or Roscommon town are equally acceptable.)\n• One named river in that region — the River Corrib, drawn flowing from Lough Corrib south into Galway Bay, and labelled. (The Moy in Mayo would serve equally.)\n\nNames may be written on the map or set out in a key. All three features after the region outline must lie inside the region you have named, or the showing marks are lost. A correct name still earns its mark where the location is inaccurate, but a feature shown without a name scores nothing at all.",
