        "question": "Explain briefly one reason why life expectancy in some countries is lower than it is in others.",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "One reason why life expectancy in some countries is lower than others is limited healthcare infrastructure: countries with fewer doctors (Sub-Saharan Africa averages 0.2 doctors per 1,000 population vs. developed nations 3-5 doctors per 1,000), fewer hospitals, and limited pharmaceutical access cannot effectively treat infectious diseases (malaria, tuberculosis, HIV affecting 100+ million in Africa) or chronic conditions, resulting in higher mortality rates and reduced life expectancy by 20-30 years compared to healthcare-rich nations. Alternative reason: poverty and malnutrition—poor populations cannot afford nutritious food, clean water, sanitation, and preventive healthcare, resulting in higher child mortality (150-200 per 1,000 in poorest regions vs. <5 per 1,000 in wealthy nations), directly reducing population life expectancy.",
