        "marks": 10,
        "model": "A suitable graph for this data is a bar chart, because the data compares a single value — the percentage of population aged over 65 — across six separate continents.\n\nHow to draw it on graph paper:\n• Title the chart 'Percentage of Population Aged Over 65 by Continent in 2018'.\n• Put the six continents — North America, Latin America, Africa, Europe, Asia, Oceania — along the horizontal axis and label it 'Continent'.\n• Put the percentage on the vertical axis, labelled '% of population over 65 years', scaled from 0 to 20 with a division every 2. The scale must begin at zero and be evenly divided.\n• Draw six bars of equal width, separated by equal gaps, of heights North America 15, Latin America 8, Africa 3, Europe 18, Asia 8 and Oceania 12.\n• Shade the bars, and add a key if they are shaded differently.\n\nMarks are awarded for the title, for labelling both axes, for an accurate and evenly divided scale, for bars of equal width and for plotting each value correctly.\n\nThe chart shows the contrast clearly: Europe, at 18%, has six times the proportion of over-65s that Africa has, at 3%.",
