      {
        "label": "A(ii)",
        "question": "Explain briefly one challenge faced by countries with high numbers of people aged over 65 years.",
        "marks": 10,
        "model": "CHALLENGE: PENSIONS AND HEALTHCARE IN AGEING POPULATIONS\n\nOne significant challenge faced by countries with high numbers of people aged over 65 years is the increased demand for healthcare services and the associated financial strain on public resources. As people age, they experience more chronic illnesses such as heart disease, diabetes, arthritis, and Alzheimer's disease, requiring more frequent medical interventions, hospitalizations, and long-term care. Japan provides a clear example: with over 29% of its population aged over 65, the country faces escalating healthcare costs for cancer treatments, dialysis, hip replacements, and dementia care. The Working-age population (aged 15-64) must support these costs through taxation and social contributions, but with fewer workers per retiree, this creates unsustainable pension and healthcare funding.\n\nFurther explanation: The dependency ratio—the number of dependents per working-age person—becomes unfavorable when the 65+ population is large. Japan's ratio is approximately 48 dependents per 100 working-age people, compared to 30-35 in younger populations. This means healthcare budgets must expand while tax bases contract, leading to government debt, higher insurance premiums, and rationing of care. Some Japanese prefectures have already closed rural hospitals due to lack of young doctors willing to work there, limiting access to care for the elderly. Additionally, long-term residential care facilities are overcrowded, waiting lists are lengthy, and family caregiving places burden on adult children (filial piety remains important in Japanese culture but is eroding with urbanization), creating social inequality in access to dignity in aging.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 16,
        "scheme_points": 10,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "question": "Discuss two issues facing cities of the future in the developed world.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "DISCUSSION: TWO ISSUES FACING CITIES OF THE FUTURE IN THE DEVELOPED WORLD\n\nISSUE 1: URBAN SPRAWL AND THE LOSS OF AGRICULTURAL LAND\n\nUrban sprawl represents one of the most pressing challenges facing developed world cities. This refers to the uncontrolled expansion of built-up areas into surrounding rural and agricultural land, driven by suburban development, car-dependent infrastructure, and speculative property investment. Cities such as London, Los Angeles, and Sydney have expanded far beyond their historical cores, consuming productive farmland and creating low-density, unsustainable settlements. The characteristic pattern shows wealthy residential suburbs (exurbia) extending 50-80 km from city centers, connected by motorways rather than integrated public transport.\n\nGeographic Consequences: Urban sprawl fragments ecosystems and destroys habitat corridors essential for biodiversity. Agricultural land, once used to feed local populations, is lost permanently. A single suburban house on a quarter-acre lot in California consumes resources that formerly supported several farming families. This creates food security issues; London now depends on imports from Spain, France, and beyond for fresh produce, making it vulnerable to supply chain disruptions and climate impacts. The sprawl pattern also requires expensive infrastructure duplication—separate water pipes, sewers, roads, and power lines for dispersed populations cost far more per capita than dense urban services.\n\nEconomic and Social Impacts: Sprawl increases commuting distances, raising transport costs and carbon emissions. A worker in the exurbs may spend 3 hours daily commuting, reducing quality of life and time for family and community. The car-dependent model concentrates wealth (car ownership is expensive; public transit-dependent poor face longer journeys), deepening social segregation. Property values in sprawling areas are volatile—when recession hits, those distant suburbs lose value fastest because they lack the walkable amenities and resilience of established urban neighborhoods.\n\nResponses Under Development: Forward-thinking cities are implementing Green Belt policies (as in London, which protects a 50 km ring of agricultural land around the city), Transit-Oriented Development (concentrating new housing within 500 m of train stations in Copenhagen and Amsterdam), and Urban Growth Boundaries (Portland, Oregon's strict limit on outward expansion has successfully redirected development to inner-city brownfield regeneration). Singapore and Vienna have pioneered mixed-use neighborhoods where residential, commercial, and recreational spaces are integrated, reducing commuting and enhancing social cohesion. However, these solutions require political will and face opposition from property developers and suburban homeowners who benefit from sprawl.\n\nISSUE 2: DIGITAL INEQUALITY AND THE CREATION OF EXCLUDED COMMUNITIES\n\nAs developed world cities increasingly become \"smart cities\" dependent on digital infrastructure—online banking, e-services, digital ID systems, algorithmic job-matching, smart transportation systems—a new form of inequality emerges: digital exclusion. Older residents, immigrants with language barriers, and low-income households lack broadband access or digital literacy, making them unable to access essential services. A study in London found that 2.3 million residents lack basic digital skills; when the UK shifted benefits applications online, thousands missed deadlines and lost support payments. In New York City, only 69% of households in low-income neighborhoods have broadband, compared to 95% in wealthy areas.\n\nThe Urban Divide: This creates a two-tier city where digitally-connected residents access faster job markets, better financial services (online banking rates are better than branch rates), and real-time transport information through apps, while the excluded face longer waits at service counters, predatory lending from check-cashing stores, and congested traditional transit systems. The algorithms used in employment websites, housing searches, and credit scoring often embed historical discrimination: if historical data shows loans defaulted more in certain zip codes, algorithms exclude those areas, perpetuating redlining. In Los Angeles, facial recognition technology used for police enforcement has been shown to misidentify people of color at higher rates, creating a surveillance and arrest bias in excluded communities.\n\nSocial Fragmentation: Smart city infrastructure designed for connected middle-class residents often ignores the needs of the excluded. Contactless payment systems in Barcelona's metro exclude unbanked migrants and asylum seekers. Digital-only education during COVID-19 locked out students without home internet, deepening achievement gaps. The rise of remote work has hollowed out city centers, reducing foot traffic that supported small businesses, cafes, and community spaces—the places where diverse groups formerly mixed. Wealthy professionals work from home in quiet suburbs; working-class service workers (cleaners, security, food service) must travel into emptied city centers to serve distant clientele, increasing their exposure to COVID-19 and other risks.\n\nResponses Emerging: Progressive cities are investing in universal broadband (Paris has made fiber optic internet a public utility). Accessible design principles are being applied—Dublin's digital services team now ensures all e-services work on phones (most accessible devices for low-income users) and offer phone/SMS alternatives to app-based systems. Community centers in excluded neighborhoods are offering free digital literacy courses. However, sustainable solutions require treating digital access as a human right and funding it as public infrastructure, not leaving it to market provision where profit-seeking ignores unprofitable populations.\n\nCONCLUSION: Both urban sprawl and digital inequality challenge developed world cities to balance growth with equity. Sprawl threatens environmental sustainability and agricultural security; digital inequality threatens social cohesion and access to opportunity. The cities succeeding in addressing these—Vienna, Amsterdam, Copenhagen, and parts of London—show that planning regulation, public investment, and inclusive design can create urban futures that work for all residents, not just the wealthy.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 16,
        "scheme_points": 30,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "Examine the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of Clonmel accompanying this paper. Using evidence from the map, account for variations in settlement distribution in the areas north and south of Northing 21.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Answer with reference to the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of Clonmel issued with this paper. The question asks for a comparison between two halves of the map, so every point should contrast the area north of Northing 21 with the area south of it.\n\nThe pattern. Settlement on this map is not evenly spread. It is concentrated on the low ground of the Suir valley and around Clonmel town, and it thins out sharply on the uplands. Because the Suir and the town lie in the northern part of the mapped area and the Comeragh mountains rise in the south, the map shows a dense, varied settlement pattern north of Northing 21 and a sparse, dispersed one south of it.\n\nNorth of Northing 21 — dense settlement. Look for the following on the map:\n• Relief: the contour lines are widely spaced and much of the land lies below 100 m in the floor of the Suir valley. Flat, well-drained land is easy and cheap to build on and easy to farm.\n• Soils and drainage: the valley floor carries deep alluvial soils deposited by the river, which support commercial farming and therefore a farming population.\n• The town: the dense grey built-up shading and close street network of Clonmel mark a substantial urban settlement with employment, schools, shops and services, which attracts population to it and to the villages around it.\n• Transport: the N24, the regional roads and the railway line converge here, and settlement follows them — the map shows lines of individual dwellings strung out along the roads, the classic linear or ribbon pattern.\n• Water supply: the River Suir provided water, power and, historically, a bridging point and a navigable route, which is why the town is sited where it is.\n\nSouth of Northing 21 — sparse settlement. Look for:\n• Relief: the contours crowd together and the land rises steeply into the Comeragh mountains, in places above 400 or 600 m. Steep slopes make building and farming difficult and roads expensive to construct, so the map shows only occasional tracks.\n• Soils and land use: the map shows extensive blanket bog, marsh symbols and coniferous plantation on the high ground. Peat is acidic, waterlogged and agriculturally worthless, and forestry is what marginal land is used for once farming becomes uneconomic; neither supports population.\n• Climate and exposure: high ground is colder, wetter and far more exposed, so crops will not ripen and living conditions are poor.\n• Accessibility: with few roads and no railway, the area is cut off from schools, shops and employment.\n• Where dwellings do occur south of Northing 21, they are dispersed — isolated farmhouses on the lower slopes and in the sheltered valleys, on the south- and east-facing ground rather than the exposed north-western slopes.\n\nHistoric evidence. The map's antiquity symbols reinforce the pattern: ringforts, souterrains and megalithic tombs cluster on the same low, dry, fertile ground that carries settlement today, showing that this distribution has held for thousands of years, while the uplands have never carried more than seasonal grazing.\n\nSummary. The variation is explained by relief, soil and drainage, climate and exposure, accessibility and the location of employment — and all five factors push in the same direction, concentrating people in the valley and emptying the mountains.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 16,
        "scheme_points": 30,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      }
