        "label": "Examine",
        "question": "Examine sustainable development as a model for future development.",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "Sustainable development represents a paradigm for future development that seeks to meet present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs. This model integrates economic growth, environmental protection, and social equity as interconnected pillars that must be balanced for long-term viability.\n\nThe economic dimension of sustainable development emphasizes the need for continued economic growth and poverty reduction while operating within planetary boundaries. Unlike traditional development models that prioritize GDP growth regardless of environmental cost, sustainable development requires economic activities to internalize environmental costs. This might involve carbon pricing mechanisms, investment in renewable energy sectors, and support for green industries. For example, Costa Rica has invested heavily in renewable energy (now generating over 99% of electricity from renewables), demonstrating that economic development and environmental protection can be complementary rather than conflicting goals.\n\nThe environmental dimension requires the protection and restoration of natural capital—forests, fisheries, freshwater systems, and the atmosphere. Sustainable development recognizes that natural resources are finite and that ecosystem services (such as pollination, water purification, and climate regulation) are essential for human survival. This necessitates moving away from linear extraction-consumption-disposal models toward circular economy principles. The Circular Economy Action Plan adopted by the EU exemplifies this shift, promoting product durability, repairability, and recycling to minimize resource depletion and waste generation.\n\nThe social dimension encompasses poverty eradication, health, education, and gender equality. Sustainable development acknowledges that development benefits must be equitably distributed across populations and generations. This includes ensuring access to quality education and healthcare, promoting gender equality (as measured by the Gender Development Index), and protecting the rights of marginalized communities. Rwanda's focus on inclusive education and women's participation in the workforce demonstrates how social development can advance alongside economic and environmental goals.\n\nGlobal governance mechanisms are essential for implementing sustainable development at scale. The United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), adopted in 2015, provide a universal framework comprising 17 interconnected goals ranging from poverty elimination to climate action. These goals recognize the interdependence of different development challenges—for instance, SDG 13 (Climate Action) intersects with SDG 2 (Zero Hunger) as climate change threatens agricultural productivity in vulnerable regions.\n\nSustainable development in practice requires institutional frameworks and multi-stakeholder collaboration. This involves strengthening environmental legislation, establishing protected areas, promoting corporate social responsibility, and fostering partnerships between governments, businesses, and civil society. The European Union's Green Deal represents a comprehensive policy framework attempting to align all policy areas—energy, transport, agriculture, circular economy—with sustainable development principles.\n\nCritics of sustainable development argue it represents a compromise that may inadequately address environmental limits or perpetuate global inequalities. However, the urgency of challenges like climate change and biodiversity loss means that purely market-driven development or command-and-control approaches have proven insufficient. Sustainable development offers a framework that maintains the possibility of continued human development while respecting planetary boundaries—a necessary model for future development in a world of finite resources and growing populations.\n\nThe transition to sustainable development requires transformative changes in energy systems, agricultural practices, consumption patterns, and technological innovation. Investment in renewable energy infrastructure, sustainable agriculture practices, and green technology transfer to developing nations are critical components. Yet this transition also demands behavior change and a fundamental reconceptualization of progress—moving from measuring development solely through GDP to metrics that capture environmental and social well-being, such as the Genuine Progress Indicator or the Human Development Index.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 18,
