        "marks": 20,
        "model": "This question requires reading specific data from an accompanying map and text showing the development of the European Union. To answer fully: (i) Identify the year the Maastricht Treaty was signed by reading the date label on the provided EU expansion timeline or text. The Treaty, formally the Treaty on European Union, should be marked on the document provided. (ii) Reference the currency information section for Bulgaria—either in the text or on a map showing historical currencies. Look for a table or legend indicating Bulgaria's former currency before euro adoption. (iii) Examine the map showing eurozone member states and identify any two countries other than Bulgaria that use the euro—these will be clearly marked or listed. Common examples include Germany, France, Spain, Italy, or other member states shown in the eurozone colour-coding. (iv) Refer to the benefits section of the text or information provided. Look for statements describing advantages of a common currency, such as eliminating exchange rate fluctuations, facilitating trade, or simplifying travel. Select two clearly stated benefits. (v) Find the text explaining reasons some EU states have not adopted the euro—this may reference convergence criteria requirements, political decisions, or economic concerns about sovereignty. Select one reason and explain its connection to a named country (e.g., Denmark's opt-out clause, Poland's economic transition requirements, or Sweden's policy choice). (vi) Examine arguments for further EU expansion in the provided text. These may include: geopolitical stability in candidate regions, economic development of peripheral areas, increased market size, or strategic positioning. Explain one benefit with reference to a named region—for example, expanding into the Balkans would enhance EU security, or including Turkey would strengthen NATO-EU coordination. The key to full marks (20 marks) is: locating exact values from the provided sources (4 marks for outline reading accuracy), answering each of the six sub-parts clearly (12 marks distributed across parts i-vi), and showing understanding of EU development processes (4 marks for explanation quality).",
