        "label": "12C",
        "question": "Examine the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map and legend accompanying this paper. Explain three reasons why the town of Dundalk developed at its location. Use evidence from the Ordnance Survey map to support each reason.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Answer with reference to the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of Dundalk issued with this paper. Each reason must be tied to something visible on the map.\n\nReason 1: a bridging point on the river, at the head of Dundalk Bay. The map shows the river flowing through the town and roads crossing it by bridge at that point. A river is an obstacle to movement, and the lowest and easiest crossing point attracts all the traffic that must cross it, so trade, markets and services grow up there. Map evidence to cite: the bridges shown in the town, and the way several roads converge on them from different directions.\n\nReason 2: a route focus and gateway position. The map shows the M1 motorway, the N1 and several regional roads meeting at or near the town, together with the railway line and station. Dundalk lies on the main Dublin–Belfast corridor and on the historic Gap of the North, the natural gateway between the two, which is why so many routes are funnelled through it. A settlement at a route focus is accessible from a wide area and therefore serves as the market and service centre for its hinterland — which is why the map shows the town with churches, schools, a hospital, a Garda station and a post office. Map evidence to cite: the number and class of routes converging, the railway line and station symbols, and the extent of the built-up shading.\n\nReason 3: a sheltered coastal site with a harbour, on flat, well-drained land. The map shows the coastline of Dundalk Bay with harbour and quay symbols, giving the town a port function for trade and fishing. The contour lines around the town are widely spaced and the land lies below about 50 m, so the site is flat, easily built on and free from the flooding and steep gradients of the uplands to the north. Map evidence to cite: the harbour symbols and the roads running to them, and the widely spaced contours beneath the built-up area contrasted with the crowded contours of the Cooley uplands.\n\nFurther supporting evidence available from the map: the good-quality farmland of the surrounding lowland, which supplied the town and gave it a market function; a water supply from the river; and the antiquity symbols in the area, showing that this ground has attracted settlement for a very long time.",
        "diagram": "",
