        "question": "State whether each of the landforms named in the table is formed by erosion or deposition or both erosion and deposition.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "The formation of river landforms involves either erosion processes, deposition processes, or both:\n\nWaterfall: Formed primarily through erosion. Vertical erosion (downcutting) is concentrated where resistant rock layers overlie softer rock, creating the vertical drop. However, some deposition can occur in the plunge pool beneath the waterfall as energy is dissipated. Primarily erosion.\n\nMeander: Formed by both erosion and deposition. The outer bank is actively eroded through lateral erosion, undercutting the meander bend. Simultaneously, the inner bank experiences deposition as slower-moving water deposits sediment to form the slip-off slope. The meander's growth involves equal parts erosion on the outer bend and deposition on the inner bend. Both erosion and deposition.\n\nFloodplain: Formed primarily through deposition. When the river exceeds bank-full capacity during flood events, water spreads across the adjacent land. The reduced velocity causes fine sediments (silt and clay) to be deposited, progressively building up the floodplain level. Deposition.\n\nDelta: Formed primarily through deposition. As the river enters standing water, velocity drops dramatically, and the river's load is deposited. Coarser sediments drop first, creating distributary channels, while finer sediments are deposited farther out. Delta growth is almost entirely a depositional process, though wave action may erode portions of existing deltas. Primarily deposition.",
