        "model": "Earth's lithospheric plates are major sections of the Earth's crust and upper mantle that move across the surface. Matching letters with named plates requires identifying their positions on a world map.\n\nThe Pacific Plate: The largest oceanic plate, underlying the Pacific Ocean. It is bounded by the Pacific Ring of Fire, an area of intense volcanic and seismic activity along its margins.\n\nThe North American Plate: Underlies most of North America and part of the Atlantic Ocean. It extends from the East Coast of North America eastward to the Mid-Atlantic Ridge.\n\nThe Eurasian Plate: The largest continental plate, underlies Europe and much of Asia. Its southern margin collides with the African and Indian plates, creating mountain ranges.\n\nThe African Plate: Underlies Africa and part of the Atlantic and Indian oceans. It is actively separating from the Eurasian Plate, with new oceanic crust being formed at the Mid-Atlantic Ridge.\n\nMatching these plates to specific letter positions on a world map would involve identifying their geographic locations and boundaries. The Pacific Plate would be identified by its position in the Pacific Ocean, the North American Plate by its position under North America, the Eurasian Plate by its position under Europe and Asia, and the African Plate by its position under Africa and the surrounding oceans.",
