        "label": "(iii)",
        "question": "Name any two river drainage patterns.",
        "marks": 2,
        "model": "Dendritic drainage patterns resemble tree-like systems with small streams joining larger streams at acute angles, commonly found in areas with uniform rock resistance and gentle slopes, such as much of Ireland. Radial drainage patterns occur where streams radiate outward from a central high point, characteristic of cone-shaped volcanoes or dome mountains. Rectangular drainage patterns develop where streams flow in right-angled patterns controlled by jointing or faulting in underlying rock. Trellis drainage patterns feature parallel main streams with tributary streams meeting at right angles, typical of areas with alternating bands of resistant and non-resistant rock. Parallel drainage patterns occur where multiple streams flow parallel to each other down uniformly-sloping terrain.",
        "diagram": "",
