        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Rock formed at A in the rock cycle diagram is igneous rock. Igneous rocks form when magma cools and solidifies, either beneath the Earth's surface or above ground following volcanic eruption. The cooling rate determines crystal size: rapid cooling produces fine-grained extrusive rocks like basalt, while slow cooling creates coarse-grained intrusive rocks like granite. Rock formed at B is sedimentary rock, which forms through lithification of weathered sediment. Sediments transported by water or wind are deposited in layers and compacted and cemented together over time, forming sedimentary rocks including sandstone, shale, and limestone. Both rock types are part of the continuous rock cycle.",
