        "model": "Rock formed at C is metamorphic rock, which develops from existing rocks subjected to heat and pressure at depth within the Earth's crust. Metamorphic rocks maintain their original chemical composition while their mineral and crystal structure changes in response to temperature and pressure increases. Examples include slate developed from shale, marble from limestone, and schist from shale or granite. In Ireland, metamorphic rocks occur in the northwest and southwest regions. The Dalradian metamorphic rocks in the northwest, particularly in County Donegal, include schist and quartzite that were metamorphosed during the Caledonian orogeny. The Ordovician metamorphic rocks at Waulsortian limestones in County Clare demonstrate metamorphic transformation in specific Irish locations.",
