        "label": "A(iv)",
        "question": "Explain briefly what is meant by the term plutonic rock.",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Plutonic rocks are igneous rocks that cool slowly beneath the Earth's surface within magma chambers deep in the crust. The slow cooling allows large mineral crystals to form, producing coarse-grained rock textures. Examples include granite, which contains visible quartz, feldspar, and mica crystals, and diorite. The slow cooling process permits complete crystallization of all magma, with different minerals crystallizing at different temperatures. Plutonic rocks eventually reach Earth's surface only after substantial uplift and erosion remove overlying rock layers. Unlike volcanic rocks that cool rapidly at the surface, plutonic rocks maintain their characteristic coarse crystallinity throughout their formation.",
