        "model": "Based on the international trade of services data for 2015, two countries with a trade surplus are the United States and the United Kingdom. A trade surplus occurs when the value of services exported exceeds the value of services imported. The United States exported significantly more services than it imported (approximately $840 billion in exports versus $580 billion in imports), creating a surplus. The United Kingdom also demonstrated a trade surplus with exports of approximately $720 billion exceeding imports of around $480 billion. Both nations, as advanced economies with strong financial, professional, and technology services sectors, generate substantial export revenues that exceed their service imports.",
