        "marks": 2,
        "model": "Net migration is the difference between immigration and emigration—calculated by subtracting the number of people leaving a country (emigration) from the number of people entering (immigration). A positive net migration occurs when immigration exceeds emigration, indicating population growth from migration. A negative net migration indicates emigration exceeds immigration, resulting in population loss. Net migration provides a summary measure of overall migration trends: the graph shows Ireland had negative net migration (more leaving than arriving) in several years, then positive net migration when immigration exceeded emigration.",
