        "label": "C",
        "question": "Answer the following question using evidence from the aerial photograph OR from the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map and legend to support your answer. Do not refer to both in your answer. Explain three reasons why the town of Macroom developed at its present location.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "**Worked Method**: To answer this using OS map evidence: (1) Identify three distinct reasons from map features at Macroom's location, each worth 10 marks requiring reason statement (2) and evidence explanation (8 marks). (2) Reason 1 - River location: The River Lee (or similar waterway) flows through Macroom's location. State: 'The river provided water supply for drinking, agriculture, and industry.' Evidence: Identify on map where river passes through settlement, note marshy areas indicating water availability. Explain: Water-powered mills used river energy; transportation by river connected to larger trade networks; water was essential before modern water systems. (3) Reason 2 - Transport and bridge crossing: The convergence of roads (N22, R584) at Macroom indicates transport advantage. State: 'Major roads converge at Macroom, making it a natural transport hub.' Evidence: Show on map how roads meet and junction at town. Explain: Crossing points over rivers became settlement locations; junction of roads created natural stopping points for traders and travellers; bridge construction attracted permanent settlements; road junction facilitated trade and commerce, supporting market development. (4) Reason 3 - Defensible position/relief: Note topography and relief features. State: 'Higher ground provided defensive advantage.' Evidence: Show on map where relief rises around town or identify position on higher ground relative to surrounding valleys. Explain: Settlement positions were strategically chosen for defence against attack (elevations providing visibility of approaches); flat areas susceptible to river flooding were avoided; position on slight elevation combined with river provided defensibility for medieval towns. Use map evidence systematically: grid references, symbols, contours, and legend to support each reason with tangible geographic evidence.",
