        "label": "Describe and account",
        "question": "Describe and account for the specific characteristics of one global climate type you have studied.",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "The temperate oceanic climate type prevalent in western Ireland, United Kingdom, and northwestern Europe exemplifies distinctive characteristics shaped by oceanic influences and prevailing westerly winds. Temperature characteristics include mild winters typically between 4-8°C despite northern latitudes (Ireland: 52-55°N) due to Gulf Stream ocean current bringing warm water northward reducing frost occurrence and enabling winter plant growth. Summers remain cool to warm with maximum temperatures typically 14-18°C, producing limited seasonal temperature range (annual range 10-14°C) compared to continental climates. Diurnal temperature variation remains limited, with oceans moderating daily temperature swings through high heat capacity. Precipitation represents defining characteristic of temperate oceanic climates, with 800-1200mm annual precipitation distributed throughout year rather than concentrated in specific season. Ireland's west coast exceeds 2000mm annually from orographic precipitation forcing moist Atlantic air upward over mountains. Precipitation originates primarily from Atlantic depressions tracking eastward across oceanic regions, bringing frontal rainfall from converging air masses. No pronounced dry season exists, enabling continuous vegetation growth. Weather variability remains high, with rapid pressure changes and frequent weather pattern shifts creating changeable conditions—frontal systems regularly bring rain followed by brief dry periods. Vegetation responds to temperate oceanic characteristics with growth of deciduous broadleaf forests including oak, ash, and beech where free drainage occurs, grasslands where wet conditions prevent tree establishment, and peat bogs in waterlogged areas. Agricultural suitability derives from reliable rainfall supporting pastoral livestock raising rather than grain cultivation. Grass growth extends year-round enabling year-round grazing impossible in continental climates with winter dormancy. These characteristics result from multiple atmospheric and oceanic factors: the Gulf Stream originating in tropical Atlantic regions brings warm water northward, transporting tropical heat poleward and raising winter temperatures 5-10°C above expected values for the latitude. Prevailing westerly winds driven by subtropical high-pressure systems and polar low-pressure systems bring moist Atlantic air masses inland, ensuring regular precipitation. Cyclonic depression formation at polar front between contrasting air masses creates weather systems tracking eastward, delivering precipitation. Orographic effects from Ireland, Scottish Highlands, and Norwegian mountains force additional uplift and precipitation. Ocean's high heat capacity moderates temperature swings, with thermal mass dampening both winter cold and summer heat. These interconnected factors create temperate oceanic climate's distinctive character, differentiating it from continental climates with wider temperature ranges and concentrated precipitation or Mediterranean climates with pronounced dry summers. The temperate oceanic climate supports affluent human societies due to productive grasslands enabling dairy farming, reliable rainfall supporting water resources, and mild winters reducing heating demands and enabling outdoor activities supporting tourism economies.",
