    "id": "geography-pp-2024-HI-section1-platetectonics-Q2",
    "subject": "geography",
    "chapterId": "geo-plate-tectonics",
    "sectionId": "geo-section1",
    "source": "LC Geography Higher 2024 Paper 2 — Q2A",
    "year": 2024,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "question": "Examine the diagram above which shows different faults and landforms created by faulting and answer each of the following questions: (i) Name each of the types of fault labelled A and B; (ii) Name each of the landforms created by faulting labelled C and D; (iii) What type of fault is found where the process of shearing occurs?; (iv) Explain briefly what causes faulting; (v) Name two periods of fold mountain building that shaped the Irish landscape over the last 400 million years; (vi) In your answerbook, state whether the following statement is true or false: A syncline is the part of a fold where the layers of rock have been pushed upwards.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "(i) A = Normal fault. B = Reverse fault.\nA normal fault is produced by tension: the crust is pulled apart, and the block of rock above the fault plane slides downwards relative to the block below. A reverse fault is produced by compression: the crust is squeezed, and the block above the fault plane is pushed upwards over the block below.\n\n(ii) C = Escarpment. D = Block mountain.\nAn escarpment (fault scarp) is the steep face left where one block has been displaced vertically against another. A block mountain, or horst, is an upstanding block left standing high between two parallel faults, either because it was pushed up or because the ground on either side dropped away; the sunken block between two faults is called a rift valley or graben.\n\n(iii) A tear fault (also called a transform, transverse or strike-slip fault). Here the two blocks slide horizontally past one another rather than moving up or down, so shearing rather than tension or compression is the force involved. The San Andreas Fault in California is the classic example.\n\n(iv) Faulting is caused by the stresses set up in the crust by plate movement. Convection currents in the asthenosphere drive the plates apart, together and past one another, and the rocks of the crust are put under tension where plates separate, under compression where they converge, and under shear where they slide past each other. Rock is brittle near the surface, so when the stress exceeds the strength of the rock it does not fold — it fractures, and the blocks on either side of the fracture move relative to one another. The sudden release of the accumulated energy at that moment is what produces an earthquake, and the seismic waves radiate outwards from the focus. Faulting is therefore closely associated with plate boundaries and is an endogenic process, driven by heat energy from inside the earth.\n\n(v) The Caledonian period, about 400 million years ago, which produced the north-east to south-west grain of Donegal, Mayo and Wicklow; and the Armorican period, about 250 million years ago, which produced the east–west ridge-and-valley structure of Munster.\n\n(vi) False. A syncline is the part of a fold where the layers of rock have been pushed downwards, forming a trough. The part of a fold where the layers have been pushed upwards into an arch is an anticline.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 4,
        "scheme_points": 6,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      }
    ]
