    "id": "geography-pp-2024-HI-section1-rocksweathering-Q3",
    "subject": "geography",
    "chapterId": "geo-rocks-weathering",
    "sectionId": "geo-section1",
    "source": "LC Geography Higher 2024 Paper 2 — Q3B",
    "year": 2024,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "B",
        "question": "Examine the formation of sedimentary rocks, with reference to examples from Ireland.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "FORMATION OF SEDIMENTARY ROCKS: IRISH EXAMPLES\n\nSedimentary rocks form through the weathering, erosion, transport, and deposition of pre-existing rock material, followed by lithification (compaction and cementation) of the accumulated sediments. Ireland provides excellent examples of different sedimentary rock types, each demonstrating distinct formation processes.\n\nDEPOSITION AND TRANSPORT PROCESSES:\nSedimentary rocks originate from mechanical weathering of parent rocks, which breaks them into smaller particles. These particles are then transported by water (rivers), ice, or wind. The size and distance a particle travels depends on transport energy—high-energy systems transport larger particles, while low-energy environments receive finer materials. This sorting by size during transport is a fundamental characteristic of sedimentary sequences.\n\nTYPE 1: SANDSTONE (CLASTIC SEDIMENTARY ROCK)\n\nFormation Process:\nSandstone forms from sand-sized particles (0.06-2 mm diameter) that are eroded from parent rocks through mechanical weathering. These sand particles are transported by rivers or coastal processes and deposited in low-energy environments—particularly river channels, floodplains, deltas, and shallow marine settings. As sand accumulates to significant thicknesses (tens to hundreds of meters), the weight of overlying sediment compacts the lower layers. Simultaneously, mineral-rich fluids (groundwater) flow through the pores between sand grains, depositing cement (typically silica, calcium carbonate, or iron oxide) that binds the grains together. This cementation is the critical process transforming loose sand into solid rock.\n\nIrish Example—The Torr Head Sandstone (County Antrim, Northern Ireland):\nThe Torr Head Sandstone is a Devonian sandstone (approximately 380-360 million years old) exposed in cliffs at Ballycastle, County Antrim. This rock is a fine-grained sandstone with a reddish-brown coloration, derived from iron oxide cement. The sandstone was deposited in a shallow marine environment during the Devonian period. The grain size indicates moderate-energy deposition—coarser than silt but finer than gravels. The massive beds visible in coastal cliffs demonstrate thickness of individual sand deposits. The presence of cross-bedding (inclined laminations) visible in the exposed cliffs indicates the action of currents during deposition, showing the sand was deposited under flowing water conditions rather than static conditions.\n\nTYPE 2: LIMESTONE (BIOCHEMICAL/CHEMICAL SEDIMENTARY ROCK)\n\nFormation Process:\nLimestone forms through the accumulation and lithification of calcium carbonate (CaCO3) material. This carbonate material originates from the shells, skeletons, and secretions of marine organisms (corals, molluscs, foraminifera, coralline algae). In shallow, warm marine environments, vast quantities of organisms produce calcium carbonate as their skeletal material. When organisms die, their shells and skeletons accumulate on the seafloor. Additionally, direct chemical precipitation of calcium carbonate can occur in warm, supersaturated seawater. Over time, shell debris and chemical precipitate accumulate in layers. Lithification occurs as the weight of overlying sediments compacts the carbonate material, and cement (additional calcium carbonate) precipitates from groundwater, binding fragments together.\n\nIrish Example—The Carboniferous Limestone (Burren Region, County Clare):\nThe Burren in County Clare is underlain by Carboniferous Limestone (approximately 350-320 million years old), which forms a distinctive karstified landscape. The limestone is thick-bedded, predominantly gray in color, and consists of fossiliferous material—visible shells and skeletal fragments of ancient marine organisms are apparent in outcrop. The limestone was deposited in a warm, shallow tropical sea during the Carboniferous period, when Ireland was located near the equator. The fossil content includes brachiopods, crinoids, and other marine invertebrates characteristic of coral-reef environments.\n\nThe Burren limestone is heavily jointed and fractured, allowing dissolution by rainwater (which is slightly acidic due to CO2 dissolution). This has created the karst topography for which the Burren is famous—sinkholes (dolines), disappearing streams, and underground caverns. The grikes (enlarged joint fissures) visible between limestone pavement blocks are widened by dissolution processes, demonstrating the ongoing chemical weathering of the limestone.\n\nTYPE 3: SHALE/MUDSTONE (CLASTIC SEDIMENTARY ROCK)\n\nFormation Process:\nShale and mudstone form from the finest sedimentary particles—silt (0.004-0.06 mm) and clay (<0.004 mm). These ultra-fine particles are produced by chemical weathering and are transported as suspended load by rivers. They are deposited in the lowest-energy environments—deep lakes, estuaries, and deep marine basins—where water flow is insufficient to transport such fine material. Over millions of years, thick accumulations of mud develop. Lithification occurs through mechanical compaction (pressure from overlying sediment squeezes out water and reduces pore space) and sometimes cementation.\n\nIrish Example—The Mweelrea Shale (County Galway, Western Ireland):\nThe Mweelrea Formation includes mudstone and shale beds of Ordovician age (approximately 480-450 million years old) exposed in the mountains of western Galway. The shale is dark gray to black, indicating deposition in oxygen-poor, deep-water environments. The dark color is due to organic content—the preserved remains of marine plankton and other organic matter that settled to the seafloor. This organic-rich shale suggests deposition in a deep marine basin with limited oxygen circulation (anoxic conditions), where organic material was preserved rather than decomposed.\n\nLITHIFICATION AND COMPACTION:\nThe transition from loose sediment to solid rock occurs through two main processes:\n\n1. Compaction: As new sediment layers accumulate on top, the weight increases pressure on lower layers. This pressure squeezes out water and reduces the pore space between grains, causing mechanical compaction. In shales, compaction can reduce thickness by 30-50%.\n\n2. Cementation: Mineral-rich groundwater flows through the sediment. As water percolates through pores, minerals precipitate and coat grain surfaces, cementing grains together. In sandstones, silica (from dissolution of quartz grains and feldspar) is the most common cement. In limestones, additional calcium carbonate precipitates.\n\nCHEMICAL COMPOSITION AND DIAGENESIS:\nAfter deposition, sediments undergo diagenesis—chemical and physical changes that transform them into rock without significant temperature/pressure increase. During diagenesis, unstable minerals (like feldspar) may dissolve, while other minerals (like calcite) may precipitate. This alters the rock's properties, sometimes creating new minerals.\n\nCONCLUSION:\nIreland's sedimentary rocks—sandstones, limestones, and shales—record diverse depositional environments from the Devonian to the Carboniferous periods. The Torr Head Sandstone reveals shallow-marine deposition with current action. The Burren Limestone documents a warm, tropical carbonate platform teeming with marine life. The Mweelrea Shale indicates deep-basin sedimentation under anoxic conditions. Each rock type preserves information about the ancient environment—water depth, energy conditions, climate, and life forms—making sedimentary rocks the primary record of Earth's environmental history.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 5,
        "scheme_points": 13,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
