    "id": "geography-pp-2024-HI-section2-urban-Q5",
    "subject": "geography",
    "chapterId": "geo-urban",
    "sectionId": "geo-section2",
    "source": "LC Geography Higher 2024 Paper 2 — Q5C",
    "year": 2024,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "Examine the factors that have influenced the development of one urban area in an Irish region that you have studied.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "FACTORS INFLUENCING THE DEVELOPMENT OF CORK CITY, IRELAND\n\nCork City, located on Ireland's southern coast at the mouth of the River Lee, is Ireland's second-largest city (population approximately 130,000 in the metropolitan area). Cork's development has been influenced by multiple interrelated factors—site and situation, economic activities, government policy, and infrastructure investment—that have shaped it into a major regional center.\n\nFACTOR 1: SITE AND SITUATION—NATURAL HARBOR AND COASTAL LOCATION\n\nCork's most fundamental developmental advantage is its natural deep-water harbor on the southern coast. The River Lee flows into Cork Harbor, which has developed into one of Ireland's major ports. The harbor's natural protection and deep-water access enable large vessel docking, making it suitable for international maritime commerce.\n\nHistorical Significance: Cork's development as a trading city began in medieval times precisely because of its harbor. The city developed as a port for trade in fish, wine, and other commodities. Viking raiders recognized the harbor's strategic importance, establishing a settlement (Norse Veðrford) that became the nucleus of modern Cork.\n\nModern Port Functions: Today, Cork Port handles approximately 8-10 million tonnes of cargo annually, making it one of Ireland's busiest ports. The port accommodates container vessels, bulk carriers, and vehicle carriers. Major imports include minerals, petroleum products, and machinery. Exports include agri-food products, pharmaceuticals, and manufactured goods.\n\nThe harbor location has enabled Cork to develop as a center for food processing, petrochemical refining, and pharmaceutical manufacturing—all industries requiring port access for raw material import and product export.\n\nFACTOR 2: GOVERNMENT POLICY AND ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT INCENTIVES\n\nGovernment support has been crucial to Cork's industrial development. The Irish government's industrial development strategy—offering tax incentives and supporting foreign direct investment—has attracted multinational corporations to Cork.\n\nPharmaceutical Manufacturing: Pfizer established a major facility in Cork in the 1970s, making Cork one of the world's largest antibiotic manufacturing centers. This decision was influenced by Irish government tax incentives (12.5% corporation tax), grant support, and industrial development programs.\n\nFood Processing: Government support for agri-food processing has enabled Cork to develop as a major food processing center. ABP (Associated British Foods), one of Europe's largest beef processors, operates major facilities in Cork, processing livestock from the surrounding agricultural hinterland.\n\nEnterprise Development: Local enterprise development agencies and government support programs have enabled small and medium enterprises in Cork to develop manufacturing, tourism, and service industries.\n\nFACTOR 3: INFRASTRUCTURE INVESTMENT\n\nModern infrastructure has been essential for Cork's contemporary economic development.\n\nRoad Networks: Investment in the M8 motorway connecting Cork to Dublin has dramatically reduced travel time and costs. The motorway enables Cork to access Dublin Port (Ireland's largest) and UK markets via ferry services. This infrastructure investment has enabled Cork companies to source raw materials and distribute products nationally and internationally.\n\nAirport Facilities: Cork Airport provides direct flights to European cities, enabling rapid transport of high-value products (pharmaceuticals) and facilitating business travel.\n\nPort Development: Continued investment in Cork Port's facilities—dredging to maintain deep-water access, container handling equipment, storage facilities—has enabled the port to handle increasingly large vessels and maintain competitiveness with other Irish ports.\n\nUtility Infrastructure: Electricity supply, water treatment, waste management, and telecommunications infrastructure enable industrial operations. Pfizer's pharmaceutical manufacturing depends on extremely pure water supply and reliable electricity.\n\nFACTOR 4: LABOUR AVAILABILITY AND EDUCATION\n\nCork's position as a university city (home to University College Cork, founded 1845) provides education and research capacity. Cork produces approximately 10,000 university graduates annually, providing educated labour for knowledge-based industries, particularly pharmaceuticals and software.\n\nManufacturing Employment: The large manufacturing sector (Pfizer, ABP, other companies) employs tens of thousands, creating a local labour market with experience in industrial operations. Labour availability in Cork has enabled continuous expansion of manufacturing.\n\nFACTOR 5: MARKET ACCESS AND HINTERLAND RESOURCES\n\nMarket Access: Cork's location on Ireland's southern coast provides relatively convenient access to UK, European, and global markets via air, sea, and land transport.\n\nAgricultural Hinterland: Cork's surrounding region is one of Ireland's most productive agricultural areas, particularly dairy and beef cattle ranching. This enables Cork's food processing industries (dairy processing, beef processing) to source raw materials locally, reducing transportation costs.\n\nFACTOR 6: HISTORICAL URBAN FUNCTIONS\n\nCork developed as a major commercial and administrative center centuries before industrialization. Pre-industrial functions—retail trade, professional services, education, religion (cathedral)—created a substantial urban population and service infrastructure that enabled Cork to transition to industrial city in the 20th century.\n\nTOURISM DEVELOPMENT:\nCork has developed tourism importance based on its historic character, natural harbor setting, and proximity to scenic attractions (West Cork coast, mountains). Tourism generates service employment (hotels, restaurants, attractions) complementing manufacturing employment.\n\nCOMPLEX INTERACTIONS:\nThese factors work synergistically. Pharmaceutical manufacturing attracts educated workers, supporting university expansion. Port infrastructure enables import of pharmaceutical raw materials and export of finished products. Government tax incentives offset geographic disadvantages relative to Dublin. Investment in roads connects Cork to national and European markets.\n\nCHALLENGES FACING CORK'S DEVELOPMENT:\n- Competition from Dublin: Dublin dominates Irish economic development, concentrating finance, government, and technology sectors there.\n- Post-Industrial Challenges: Some traditional manufacturing has declined (textiles, shipbuilding), requiring economic restructuring.\n- Labour Emigration: Young educated people often migrate to Dublin or abroad, reducing local labour availability.\n- Infrastructure Limitations: While improved, Cork's transport connections remain secondary to Dublin's.\n\nCONCLUSION:\nCork's development has been shaped by interconnected factors: a natural harbor enabling port-based commerce, government economic development policies, infrastructure investment, labour availability and education, market access, and historical urban functions. Understanding Cork's development requires recognizing how these factors reinforce each other, creating a complex urban system adapted to geographic opportunities and supported by government policy and investment.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 7,
        "scheme_points": 10,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
