        "label": "B",
        "question": "Examine the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map and legend accompanying this paper and answer each of the following questions: (i) Explain two reasons why a fish processing plant might choose to locate in the area covered by the map. Use evidence from the Ordnance Survey map to support each reason; (ii) Using evidence from the Ordnance Survey map, explain one reason why a fish processing plant might choose not to locate in this area.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Answer with reference to the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of Bantry issued with this paper. Each point must be tied to something actually visible on the map.\n\n(i) Two reasons a fish processing plant might locate in the area.\n\nReason 1 — the raw material is landed here. A fish processing plant must be as close as possible to the catch, because fish is highly perishable and loses value with every hour it spends in transit. The map shows the coastline of Bantry Bay, a deep, sheltered natural inlet, with harbour, pier and quay symbols marking where boats can land, and the legend's symbols for fish farms or shellfish beds where these occur. The bay is also long, deep and protected from Atlantic swell, which is why aquaculture — mussel and salmon farming — is established in it. Locating at the quayside means the catch goes straight from boat to plant. Map evidence to cite: the harbour and pier symbols, the shape and depth of the bay, and any aquaculture markings.\n\nReason 2 — flat, well-drained land beside the town with road access. The map shows the built-up area of Bantry on low ground where the contour lines are widely spaced, and the N71 national secondary route and the R584 regional road running through it. A processing plant needs a large, level site for a single-storey building, chilled storage, yards and lorry turning, and it needs a road link to get the finished product to Cork, to Dublin and to the ferry and air freight routes for export within hours. The town also supplies a workforce, an existing water supply — essential for washing and processing — a sewerage system and an electricity supply, all of which the plant can connect to rather than provide. Map evidence to cite: the widely spaced contours on the low ground, the class and number of the roads, and the extent of the built-up shading.\n\n(ii) One reason a fish processing plant might choose not to locate in the area.\n\nPeripherality and distance from market. The map shows only a national secondary route and regional roads; there is no motorway, no railway line and no airport within the mapped area. West Cork is one of the most remote parts of the State from Dublin Port and Dublin Airport, and from the European market beyond them. For a product that must reach the customer within days, the extra hours on narrow, winding roads through the peninsulas raise transport costs and cut shelf life, and the same remoteness makes it harder to bring in packaging and equipment. That is why large-scale food processing has tended to concentrate closer to the main road corridors.\n\nOther reasons that can be supported from the map: the small size of the settlement, which limits the pool of skilled labour available locally; the risk of exposure and storm damage on a west-facing Atlantic coast; the steep, indented terrain shown by crowded contours around the bay, which restricts the number of large flat sites; and conflict with tourism, since the map's caravan, camping, viewpoint and scenic-route symbols show an area whose economy depends on its scenery and amenity, and a processing plant brings smell, effluent and heavy traffic.",
