    "id": "geography-pp-2024-HI-section3-economicactivities-Q9",
    "subject": "geography",
    "chapterId": "geo-economic-activities",
    "sectionId": "geo-section3",
    "source": "LC Geography Higher 2024 Paper 2 — Q9A",
    "year": 2024,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "question": "Examine the data above showing the unemployment rate for 25 to 74 year olds in Ireland for each year, 2018 to 2023. Answer each of the following questions: (i) Using graph paper, draw a suitable graph to illustrate this data; (ii) Explain briefly one way that high employment benefits the Irish economy.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "IRELAND'S UNEMPLOYMENT RATE (2018-2023): GRAPH AND ECONOMIC BENEFITS\n\nPART (i): DRAWING A SUITABLE GRAPH\n\nData for Graphing:\nYear | Unemployment Rate (%)\n2018 | 4.8\n2019 | 4.1\n2020 | 4.4\n2021 | 5.0\n2022 | 4.2\n2023 | 4.0\n\nAppropriate Graph Type—Line Graph:\nA line graph is most suitable for illustrating unemployment trends over time. This graph type clearly shows changes and patterns across the 6-year period.\n\nGraph Components (16 marks total):\n\n1. Title (2 marks): A clear, descriptive title such as \"Unemployment Rate for 25-74 Year Olds in Ireland, 2018-2023\" or \"Trends in Irish Unemployment, 2018-2023.\" The title must reference the chart content and can appear anywhere on the graph.\n\n2. Vertical Axis (Y-axis) Label (1 mark): Labeled \"Unemployment Rate (%)\" with numerical scale from 0-6% (or 0-7%) with clear increment markings (e.g., 0%, 1%, 2%, 3%, 4%, 5%, 6%).\n\n3. Horizontal Axis (X-axis) Label (1 mark): Labeled \"Year\" with years marked from 2018 to 2023.\n\n4. Data Points Plotted (6 marks total, graded 2/1/0):\nFor each year, plot the unemployment percentage as a point on the graph:\n- 2018: 4.8% (plot at the intersection of 2018 and 4.8%)\n- 2019: 4.1% (plot at 2019, 4.1%)\n- 2020: 4.4% (plot at 2020, 4.4%)\n- 2021: 5.0% (plot at 2021, 5.0%)\n- 2022: 4.2% (plot at 2022, 4.2%)\n- 2023: 4.0% (plot at 2023, 4.0%)\n\nGrading: 2 marks for accurate plotting; 1 mark for minor inaccuracies; 0 marks if significantly misplaced.\n\n5. Line Connection (2 marks): Connect the six plotted points with straight lines to show the trend over time.\n\nMarking Criteria:\n- Graph paper must be used (deduct 2 marks if not used)\n- Axis labels and increments must be clear and accurately scaled\n- Data points must be accurately positioned\n- Line connections must be straight between points\n- Title must reference chart content\n\nGraph Interpretation:\nThe resulting line graph shows:\n- Decline from 2018 (4.8%) to 2019 (4.1%): unemployment decreased\n- Slight increase from 2019 to 2020 (4.4%): minor rise, likely due to COVID-19 economic disruption\n- Peak in 2021 (5.0%): highest unemployment in the period\n- Sustained decline from 2021 through 2023: unemployment decreased to 4.0%\n- Overall trend: unemployment fluctuated within a narrow range (4.0%-5.0%), indicating relative labor market stability\n\nPART (ii): HOW HIGH EMPLOYMENT BENEFITS THE IRISH ECONOMY\n\nHigh employment (low unemployment rates) benefits the Irish economy through multiple mechanisms:\n\nOne Key Benefit—Increased Tax Revenue and Reduced Welfare Spending:\n\nTax Revenue Generation:\nWhen unemployment is low, more people are employed and earning wages. Employed workers pay income tax to the Irish government. Lower unemployment rates mean more taxpayers. For example, if the unemployment rate decreased from 5.0% (2021) to 4.0% (2023) in a labor force of approximately 2.5 million people, this represents approximately 25,000 additional employed people. At an average income of €40,000 annually and a 20% income tax rate, this generates approximately €200 million in additional annual tax revenue. Employed workers also pay employer/employee social security contributions, further increasing government revenue.\n\nReduced Welfare Expenditure:\nUnemployed people receive unemployment benefits (social welfare payments) from the government. When unemployment is low, fewer people require these benefits, reducing government spending. Ireland spends approximately €1-2 billion annually on unemployment benefits. Each percentage-point reduction in unemployment saves the government approximately €50-100 million in benefit payments. This combined effect—increased tax revenue plus reduced benefit spending—creates approximately €250-300 million annual fiscal benefit for each percentage-point reduction in unemployment.\n\nEconomic Consequences:\nIncreased government revenue enables investment in public services (healthcare, education, infrastructure) that support economic growth and quality of life. Alternatively, reduced welfare spending allows tax reductions that stimulate consumer spending and business investment.\n\nAlternative Benefits (Brief Explanation):\n\nConsumer Spending and Economic Growth:\nEmployed workers have income enabling consumption. Low unemployment means more consumers with spending power. Consumer spending represents approximately 50-60% of Irish GDP. When employment is high, consumer confidence increases, and consumer spending grows, stimulating business revenue and encouraging business investment and job creation—a virtuous cycle of economic expansion.\n\nLabor Market Stability and Business Investment:\nLow unemployment indicates a stable, growing labor market. Businesses confident in labor availability invest in expansion, knowing they can hire workers. This business investment creates additional jobs and economic growth.\n\nReduced Social Problems:\nHigh unemployment creates social problems (poverty, mental health issues, crime, social discord). By reducing these problems, high employment reduces associated government costs (healthcare, law enforcement, social services) and improves quality of life and social stability.\n\nConclusion:\nHigh employment benefits the Irish economy primarily through increased tax revenue and reduced welfare spending, which enables government investment in public services and economic development. Additional benefits include increased consumer spending stimulating economic growth, business investment, and improved social stability.",
        "diagram": "exam-images/geography/2024-HL-Q9.png",
        "source_page": 12,
        "scheme_points": 6,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
