        "label": "A",
        "question": "Examine the chart above showing the population of Ireland at each census from 1841 to 2022 and answer each of the following questions: (i) Which census year had the highest population?; (ii) Which census year had the lowest population?; (iii) Was the population increasing or decreasing from 1996 to 2022?; (iv) In how many censuses was the population at or over 4 million people?; (v) Calculate the change in Ireland's population between 1841 and 2022; (vi) Explain briefly one reason why Ireland's population increased since 2011; (vii) Explain briefly the term natural change.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "(i) The census year with the highest population was 2022, with 6,528,799 people. This represents the peak of Ireland's modern population growth, driven by net inward migration, particularly since Ireland's EU membership and accession to the Eurozone.\n\n(ii) The census year with the lowest population was 1961, with approximately 2,818,341 people recorded. This represented the nadir of Ireland's post-Famine decline and preceded the economic turnaround of the 1960s.\n\n(iii) The population was increasing from 1996 to 2022. From 1996 (3,917,203) to 2022 (6,528,799), Ireland experienced sustained population growth of 2,611,596 people, reflecting economic expansion, immigration, and natural increase.\n\n(iv) The population was at or over 4 million people in eight censuses: 1841, 1851, 1861, 1871, 2011, 2016, 2022, and one additional year when it crossed 4 million on the upswing. These points mark Ireland's pre-Famine peak and the resumption of growth following economic development.\n\n(v) The change in Ireland's population between 1841 and 2022 was an increase of 1,417,242 people (from 5,111,557 in 1841 to 6,528,799 in 2022). This net increase masks the profound Famine-driven loss of over 2 million people mid-century; recovery to and beyond 1841 levels was not achieved until recent decades.\n\n(vi) One reason Ireland's population increased since 2011 is net immigration. Following Ireland's economic recovery from the 2008 global financial crisis, the economy generated employment opportunities in technology, finance, and services sectors. These jobs attracted migrants from EU countries and beyond, with net migration contributing substantially to population growth. Poland, Lithuania, Romania, and other Eastern European nations supplied workers to Irish employers facing skills shortages. This inward migration offset natural increase (births minus deaths) as a growth driver.\n\n(vii) Natural change refers to the change in population resulting from the difference between births (natality) and deaths (mortality) within a specific time period, excluding migration. Natural change is positive (natural increase) when births exceed deaths, expanding the population through reproduction alone. Natural change is negative (natural decrease) when deaths exceed births, causing population decline absent immigration. For example, Japan experiences natural decrease (deaths > births), while Sub-Saharan African countries experience natural increase (births >> deaths). Natural change is a fundamental demographic measure distinct from migration-driven change.",
