        "label": "C",
        "question": "Discuss two challenges facing cities of the future.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "CHALLENGE 1: CLIMATE CHANGE, ENVIRONMENTAL DEGRADATION, AND SUSTAINABILITY\n\nClimate change presents an unprecedented challenge to cities of the future. Rising global temperatures drive sea-level rise, increased flooding, and extreme weather events. Coastal cities like New York, Venice, Miami, and Manila face existential threats from permanent inundation. Storm surge during hurricanes and typhoons will worsen; hurricane-induced flooding in New Orleans (2005, Hurricane Katrina) caused over 1,000 deaths and $125 billion in damages, exemplifying urban climate vulnerability. Future cities will experience more intense heat waves—the 2003 European heat wave killed 70,000 people, mostly in cities where urban heat island effects concentrate heat. Cities must invest in climate adaptation: sea walls and flood management (Rotterdam's Room for the River project demonstrates adaptation), cooling centres, green infrastructure, and drainage systems. Simultaneously, cities contribute 75% of global carbon emissions through buildings, transport, and services; transitioning to renewable energy, electrifying transport, and retrofitting buildings requires massive investment. Mumbai, with 20 million people in a vulnerable coastal zone, faces triple climate threats: sea-level rise, monsoon intensification, and heat stress. Middle Eastern cities like Dubai face extreme heat making outdoor work impossible in summer months. Adaptation costs are astronomical; Singapore spends billions on flood defences and desalination. This challenge requires green infrastructure (urban forests, permeable pavements), compact mixed-use development reducing transport, circular economy principles, and international climate agreements. Cities must balance climate mitigation (reducing emissions) and adaptation (preparing for unavoidable change) while maintaining economic viability and social equity.\n\nCHALLENGE 2: HOUSING AFFORDABILITY CRISIS AND INEQUALITY\n\nHousing affordability represents a defining urban challenge of the future. Rapid urbanisation, restricted housing supply, foreign investment, gentrification, and wage stagnation have created unprecedented affordability crises in major cities. London: median house prices are 13 times average salary, making homeownership impossible for most residents. Vancouver: foreign investment and speculation inflated housing prices; a typical home costs C$800,000+ while median income is C$65,000. San Francisco: tech sector growth attracted workers but housing supply stagnated; homelessness increased dramatically despite high average incomes. Dublin: construction collapsed post-2008; insufficient housing supply combines with foreign institutional investment in buy-to-rent, pushing rents and prices beyond local wage levels. This affordability crisis creates spatial segregation: wealthy professionals occupy gentrified inner cities while lower-income residents are displaced to distant suburbs. This produces long commutes, reduced quality of life, and exacerbates inequality. Homelessness increases even in wealthy cities; rough sleeping visible in London, New York, San Francisco, and Dublin creates social pathology and wasted human potential. Tokyo provides a counter-example: cultural acceptance of apartment living, land-value taxes limiting speculation, and proactive public housing keep affordability manageable. Future cities must address housing through diverse strategies: public housing investment (Vienna has 60% social/subsidised housing), zoning reform allowing denser mixed-income development, rent controls or stabilisation, taxes on vacant properties and foreign speculation, and community land trusts. Without addressing housing, future cities will become economically segregated, with service workers unable to afford living near employment, creating transport pressure and social instability. Barcelona, experiencing intense tourist-driven gentrification and housing crisis, demonstrates the urgent need for pro-affordability policy intervention.",
