        "label": "A",
        "question": "Examine the historical map of Bantry shown above. Draw a sketch map of the historical map. On it, correctly show and label each of the following: Bantry Harbour; Two connecting roads/streets; The school; The court house.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Answer with reference to the historical map of Bantry printed on the paper.\n\nDraw a sketch of the historical map with four ruled sides, roughly the same proportions as the original, and locate each feature by eye so that it is in approximately the right position and proportion relative to the others. Label every feature on the sketch itself or in a key.\n\nWhat to show and label:\n• Bantry Harbour — outlined as the water area on the seaward side of the map and labelled.\n• Two connecting roads/streets — a connecting street is one that joins two other streets at both ends, so choose two that clearly do so rather than dead ends. Draw each as a double line and label both.\n• The school — marked as a small block in its correct position and labelled, using the name printed on the historical map.\n• The court house — marked as a small block in its correct position and labelled.\n\nEgerton Square and the named street shown on the historical map are useful reference points for placing the other features correctly.\n\nMarks: 4 for the sketch outline, then 4 for each of the four items — showing it in approximately the correct position and proportion, and labelling it. A feature that is shown but not labelled scores nothing for showing, and tracing the historical map rather than sketching it loses the outline marks.",
