    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "",
        "question": "Discuss how conflict may develop between economic interests and environmental interests as a result of the exploitation of resources, with reference to two examples that you have studied.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Oil and natural gas exploitation in the Niger Delta region of Nigeria exemplifies how fundamental conflicts develop between economic development interests and environmental protection, making resolution extraordinarily difficult.\n\nECONOMIC INTERESTS DOMINATING DEVELOPMENT: The Niger Delta contains vast proven oil reserves (approximately 37 billion barrels) making it globally significant. International oil corporations (Royal Dutch Shell, ExxonMobil, Chevron, Eni) pursued aggressive exploration and production to maximise shareholder returns and supply global markets. The Nigerian government depends on oil revenues for approximately 95% of export earnings and 80% of government revenues, creating structural economic dependence. This revenue dependence shapes government policy, incentivising extraction over environmental protection. Local communities initially anticipated employment and development benefits would accompany resource extraction. These converging economic imperatives created powerful incentives prioritising extraction operations over environmental considerations.\n\nENVIRONMENTAL IMPACTS CREATING CONFLICT: Oil spills from corroded pipelines and production accidents contaminated soil throughout the region, destroying agricultural productivity. Fishing communities faced fish stock decimation from water pollution. Gas flaring (burning associated natural gas) released methane and carbon dioxide, contributing to climate change while wasting resources that could generate revenue. Deforestation for pipeline routes and facility construction destroyed rainforest biomes and forest-dependent communities' livelihoods. Water pollution from petroleum products, chemical discharge, and produced water made surface and groundwater unfit for consumption; communities relied on contaminated water sources causing health problems. Air pollution from flaring and emissions caused respiratory diseases. Acid rain resulted from nitrogen oxides in flaring emissions. Biodiversity loss eliminated species and disrupted ecosystems.\n\nDEVELOPMENT OF CONFLICT: This created direct, irreconcilable conflict between economic interests and environmental protection. Affected communities demanded environmental remediation and compensation, directly contradicting company profit maximisation. Government revenue dependence meant environmental regulations, though formally existing, faced weak enforcement. Environmental activist movements emerged (Ken Saro-Wiwa's Movement for the Survival of the Ogoni People—MOSOP) opposing extraction; Saro-Wiwa's execution by the Nigerian military demonstrated conflict intensity. International environmental organisations (Greenpeace, Environmental Rights Action) documented impacts, contradicting industry sustainability claims. The fundamental incompatibility arose because resolving environmental degradation would require abandoning extraction—unacceptable given government revenue dependence and company profit motivations.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 22,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
