        "label": "",
        "question": "Examine the impact of two of the following on the development of footloose industries or financial services or mass tourism in a developed economy that you have studied: Physical factors, Human factors, Political factors.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Switzerland exemplifies how physical, human, and political factors influence the development of footloose industries and financial services in a developed economy.\n\nPHYSICAL FACTORS: Switzerland's location at the intersection of Alpine regions creates geographic centrality to major European markets (Germany, Italy, France), reducing distribution costs. Alpine topography historically discouraged large-scale agriculture, pushing economic development toward high-value services and precision manufacturing. Limited natural resources (beyond hydropower and timber) forced reliance on imported materials, encouraging processing industries adding value. Hydropower abundance provided cheap electricity supporting energy-intensive industries. Good climate for tourism (Alpine scenery, skiing) developed the hospitality sector.\n\nHUMAN FACTORS: Switzerland developed a highly educated, multilingual workforce speaking German, French, Italian, and English. Strong educational institutions (ETH Zurich, University of Zurich) produced engineers and scientists. Cultural emphasis on precision, reliability, and quality gave Swiss manufacturing global reputation. Banking tradition dating to medieval times created financial expertise and capital accumulation. Strong work ethic and low corruption ratings enhanced business confidence. Immigration policies selectively admitted skilled workers, maintaining human capital advantages.\n\nPOLITICAL FACTORS: Political stability and neutrality (not joining NATO or EU) enhanced Switzerland's appeal to multinational companies seeking secure locations. Strong intellectual property protections attracted pharmaceutical research and development (Novartis, Roche). Low corporate tax rates (particularly in cantons like Zug) attracted financial services and headquarters functions. Stringent banking secrecy laws historically attracted wealth management but recently weakened through international pressure. Democratic governance and rule of law provided predictable business environments. Currency stability (Swiss franc) and sound monetary policy (Swiss National Bank independence) created confidence. Regulatory frameworks supported innovation in pharmaceuticals, chemicals, and precision engineering.\n\nRESULT: Switzerland hosts headquarters of major multinational corporations, particularly in pharmaceuticals (Novartis, Roche), chemicals (Syngenta), and financial services. Zurich and Geneva emerged as global financial centres. Precision manufacturing (watches, machinery) achieved global dominance. Tourism developed as major economic sector.",
