        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Two effects of increasing old age dependency ratio:\n\n(1) FISCAL PRESSURE: Governments must fund pensions and elderly healthcare from tax revenues. As the elderly population increases relative to working-age taxpayers, governments face pressure to either increase taxes on workers, reduce pension benefits, or raise retirement ages. Countries with unfunded pension systems (paying current retirees from current tax revenues) face acute fiscal crises. Ireland's public pension expenditure is projected to increase from approximately 4% of GDP (2015) to 7% by 2050.\n\n(2) LABOUR SUPPLY CONSTRAINTS: As the working-age population shrinks relative to total population, labour shortages emerge in many sectors. Wages rise, increasing business costs. Healthcare and social care sectors face particular labour shortages as demand for elderly care increases simultaneously with reduced working-age population availability. Economies must either increase immigration to compensate for labour shortages or automate more functions, disrupting employment patterns.",
