        "question": "Discuss two issues facing cities of the future in the developed world.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Two major issues facing cities of the future in the developed world include sustainability and inequality.\n\nSUSTAINABILITY CHALLENGE: Cities consume approximately 80% of global energy despite occupying 2% of earth's surface, driving climate change through greenhouse gas emissions (transport, heating/cooling, industrial processes). Waste generation (each urban resident generates 1-2 kg waste daily) creates landfill problems and resource depletion. Water scarcity threatens many cities; Los Angeles, Phoenix, and Middle Eastern cities face acute water stress as aquifers deplete. Automobile-dependent urban forms create sprawl consuming agricultural land and increasing commute distances. Climate change threatens cities directly through flooding (rising sea levels, increased storm surge), heat waves (urban heat island effects), and drought (water supply disruption). Future sustainability requires decarbonising energy (renewable sources), transitioning to circular economy (recycling, reduced consumption), sustainable transport (public transit, cycling), and adaptation to climate impacts.\n\nINEQUALITY CHALLENGE: Urban inequality intensifies as wealthy professionals congregate in expensive central areas or exclusive suburbs while poor populations concentrate in sprawling informal settlements (favelas, slums). Income inequality within cities exceeds national averages; Mumbai's richest 10% earn 40+ times the poorest 10%. Segregated schooling perpetuates inequality; children in poor neighbourhoods attend under-resourced schools limiting social mobility. Healthcare disparities; wealthy neighbourhoods have modern hospitals while poor areas lack basic services. Homelessness and housing insecurity expand as housing costs increase (London, San Francisco, Sydney median house prices exceed middle-income purchasing power). Police violence and incarceration disproportionately affect poor and minority communities. Gentrification displaces long-resident communities as neighbourhood investment increases property values. These inequalities create social tension, reduced social cohesion, and political instability threatening urban viability.",
