      {
        "label": "",
        "question": "As cities grow and expand they impact on the surrounding rural areas. Discuss this statement with reference to example(s) that you have studied.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Cities growing and expanding have profound impacts on surrounding rural areas, creating interconnected urban-rural relationships.\n\nURBAN SPRAWL IMPACTS: Urban expansion consumes agricultural land at periphery; farmland directly adjacent to cities is converted to residential subdivisions and commercial centres. Ireland's Cork and Dublin peripheries lost thousands of hectares of productive agricultural land since 1990. This reduces national food production capacity and destroys farming communities' livelihoods as agricultural land values collapse (conversion value exceeds agricultural value 10-fold). Rural character is lost as villages merge into suburban sprawl, eroding distinctive rural community identities. Infrastructure costs increase for water, sewage, and electricity provision across sprawled areas compared to dense urban cores.\n\nRURAL DEMOGRAPHIC IMPACTS: Young people migrate from surrounding rural areas toward cities seeking employment in service sectors unavailable rurally. Rural villages experience population decline and ageing as youth depart; village schools close (Ireland's rural school closures numbered 600+ since 2000). Social services concentrate in cities; rural residents travel long distances to hospitals, third-level education, and specialist services. Agricultural employment declines as mechanisation and consolidation concentrate farms; family farming becomes economically unviable. Rural communities lose social cohesion as churches, post offices, and shops close due to population loss.\n\nENVIRONMENTAL IMPACTS: Agricultural intensification occurs on farmland remaining at urban periphery; farmers intensify production (chemical inputs, machinery) on smaller holdings to remain economically viable. Hedgerow removal and field consolidation for intensive agriculture reduce biodiversity at landscape scale. Water pollution from suburban development (septic systems, runoff) affects rural water quality. Green spaces at urban periphery are lost to development despite planning protections. Recreational pressure increases as urban residents drive to rural areas for recreation (hiking, fishing), creating congestion and environmental stress.\n\nECONOMIC INTEGRATION: Surrounding rural areas become dependent on urban centres economically. Farmers sell produce to urban markets; rural employment increasingly involves commuting to urban jobs. Rural land values rise speciously due to development potential; actual agricultural productivity becomes secondary to land speculation. Small rural businesses cannot compete with urban chain stores; rural retail services decline. However, tourism and recreation in rural areas near cities can generate income replacing lost agriculture.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 28,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
