        "question": "Describe and explain the general composition and characteristics of any one soil type.",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "Describe and explain the general composition and characteristics of any one soil type. [80 marks]\n\nPODZOL SOIL (Boreal/Temperate Region): Podzols develop under coniferous forests in cool climates with significant precipitation. These soils display distinct characteristics reflecting their environment and processes.\n\nCOMPOSITION: Podzols are dominated by quartz sand derived from weathering of silica-rich parent materials (granite, sandstone). Organic matter accumulates as acidic humus (mor) in surface layers; thick organic horizon (O horizon) reflects slow decomposition in cool climate. Iron and aluminium compounds, leached from upper horizons, accumulate in lower horizons creating characteristic reddish/orange coloured B horizon (illuvial layer).\n\nCHARACTERISTICS: Podzols are highly acidic (pH 3.5-4.5) due to organic acid production and base cation leaching. Fertility is low; nutrient bases (calcium, potassium) are leached into groundwater or lower horizons, unavailable to plant roots. Structure is loose and crumbly; low clay content provides minimal binding. Drainage is excessive in sandy podzols; water percolates rapidly. These characteristics reflect intense leaching in high-rainfall environments and slow decomposition in cool climate.\n\nFORMATION PROCESSES: Cool climate temperatures limit decomposition; organic matter accumulates in thick O horizon. Acidic organic acids from leaf litter percolate downward, causing eluviation of iron, aluminium, and organic compounds from upper horizons (A horizon bleaches white/grey). These compounds are transported to B horizon where they accumulate, creating distinctive orange/red coloured layer. This process continues slowly over centuries, creating mature podzol profiles metres deep.\n\nDISTRIBUTION AND USE: Podzols cover extensive areas in Russia, Scandinavia, Canada, and northern USA under coniferous forests. They support forest ecosystems but require intensive management for agriculture; acidic conditions must be corrected through liming (calcium addition). Most remain forested; agricultural use is limited.",
        "diagram": "",
