        "label": "",
        "question": "Describe the formation of each of the following and discuss its impact on weather patterns: Mid-latitude depressions, Anticyclones, Land and sea breezes.",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "Describe the formation of each of the following and discuss its impact on weather patterns: Mid-latitude depressions, Anticyclones, Land and sea breezes. [80 marks]\n\nMID-LATITUDE DEPRESSIONS: Low-pressure weather systems form where warm tropical air masses collide with cold polar air masses, typically along frontal boundaries around 40-60° latitude. Formation process: Two air masses of different temperature/humidity meet along convergent zones (jet streams create boundaries where cold polar and warm subtropical air collide); the warmer air mass is less dense and rises above the colder, denser air. This upward motion creates low pressure at the surface (the depression/low). Air converges toward the low-pressure centre, spiralling counterclockwise (Northern Hemisphere) or clockwise (Southern Hemisphere) due to Coriolis effect. The rising air cools, causing water vapour condensation and precipitation.\n\nWEATHER IMPACTS: Depressions bring unstable weather—clouds, rain, strong winds. Warm fronts associated with depressions bring light rain gradually increasing intensity as the warm air mass overtakes cooler air. Cold fronts bring intense rain/thunderstorms as cold air undercuts warm air, forcing vigorous uplift. Wind speeds are strong around depressions; pressure gradient (difference between high and low pressure areas) creates strong winds. Weather is changeable; a location experiences weather changes as depression passes—improving conditions behind cold front, deteriorating conditions ahead of warm front. Depressions move westerly to easterly (Northern Hemisphere), bringing weather changes sequentially across regions.\n\nANTICYCLONES (HIGH-PRESSURE SYSTEMS): Anticyclones form when dense cold air sinks at high latitudes (polar regions, upper atmosphere) or when air masses stagnate at the surface. Formation: Sinking air creates high pressure at the surface (anticyclone/high). Air diverges outward from high-pressure centre, flowing clockwise (Northern Hemisphere) or counterclockwise (Southern Hemisphere) due to Coriolis effect. Sinking air warms adiabatically (temperature increases without heat input); this warming reduces cloud formation by decreasing relative humidity. Anticyclones are stable systems; once formed, they resist change and can persist for weeks.\n\nWEATHER IMPACTS: Anticyclones bring stable, settled weather—clear skies, little precipitation, moderate winds. Summer anticyclones bring heat waves with temperature extremes; daily maximum temperatures exceed 30-35°C. Winter anticyclones bring clear cold nights with ground frost; prolonged cold can create harsh conditions. Absence of clouds permits high daytime heating and rapid night-time cooling (lack of cloud insulation). Winds are light near the high-pressure centre; extreme anticyclones (blocking highs) stall weather patterns, creating stagnant conditions persisting for weeks. Extended drought results if anticyclones persist during growing season—blocking highs prevented rainfall during European droughts (2003, 2015).\n\nLAND AND SEA BREEZES: Local wind systems result from differential heating of land and ocean. Formation—Day time: Land heats rapidly due to lower heat capacity and higher solar absorption; ocean heats slowly due to higher heat capacity and water mixing. Warmer air over land rises, creating low pressure at surface. Air flows from high-pressure ocean toward low-pressure land (sea breeze), creating onshore flow. This circulation is thermally direct (driven by temperature/pressure differences, not Coriolis effect).\n\nNight time: Land cools rapidly due to rapid radiation loss (lack of clouds permits rapid heat loss); ocean retains heat due to high heat capacity. Cooler air over land creates high pressure at surface; warm ocean air rises, creating low pressure. Air flows from high-pressure land toward low-pressure ocean (land breeze), creating offshore flow.\n\nWEATHER IMPACTS: Sea breezes moderate daytime coastal temperatures, creating cooler conditions at coast than inland. This brings welcome relief during hot summers; coastal areas experience temperatures 3-5°C cooler than inland areas during sea breezes. Sea breezes increase humidity; maritime air carries moisture, bringing clouds and occasional precipitation to coastal regions. Land breezes create offshore flow, clearing coastal clouds and precipitation. These local circulations can be overridden by larger-scale pressure patterns; strong anticyclones can suppress sea breezes. Sea/land breezes are most pronounced during high-pressure conditions (anticyclones) when larger-scale winds are light.",
