        "question": "Examine one distinctive global climate type that you have studied with reference to three of the following: Temperature, Precipitation, Location, Pressure belts (air circulation).",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "Examine one distinctive global climate type that you have studied with reference to three of the following: Temperature, Precipitation, Location, Pressure belts (air circulation). [80 marks]\n\nTROPICAL RAINFOREST CLIMATE (Equatorial Climate)\n\nLOCATION: Tropical rainforest climates are located between approximately 10°N and 10°S latitude, concentrated in three major regions: Amazon Basin (Brazil, Peru, Colombia), Congo Basin (Democratic Republic of Congo, Central African Republic), and Southeast Asian islands (Indonesia, Malaysia). Smaller areas exist in parts of West Africa, Madagascar, and northeastern Australia. These locations are near the equator where Hadley cells (atmospheric circulation patterns) create rising air and convergence zones (Intertropical Convergence Zone—ITCZ).\n\nTEMPERATURE CHARACTERISTICS: Tropical rainforest regions experience consistently warm temperatures throughout the year. Mean annual temperature is typically 24-28°C; temperature variation is minimal—monthly mean temperatures typically range <3°C annually. There is no winter season; even \"coolest\" months remain warm. Diurnal (daily) temperature range is often greater than annual range; daily highs may reach 32-35°C while nights cool to 20-22°C. This consistent warmth reflects consistent solar radiation input year-round at equatorial latitudes; the sun's angle is high throughout the year, creating strong insolation. Temperature remains high due to atmospheric humidity absorbing and reradiating longwave radiation.\n\nPRECIPITATION CHARACTERISTICS: Tropical rainforest climates receive abundant precipitation, typically 2000-10000mm annually; some locations exceed 10000mm. Crucially, precipitation is distributed year-round; there is no dry season. Rainfall occurs daily or nearly daily, particularly in afternoon when convectional heating maximises. Heavy daily thunderstorms are characteristic weather. Annual rainfall is reliable; year-to-year variation is modest. Precipitation is generated through convectional processes—strong solar heating causes air mass instability; rising saturated air condenses at low altitudes (500-1500m), creating daily thunderstorms. The ITCZ brings convergent winds year-round, maintaining this convectional precipitation.\n\nPRESSURE BELT (AIR CIRCULATION): Tropical rainforest climates exist within the Hadley cell circulation system. Air rises at the ITCZ (equatorial low pressure), creating convergent winds from both hemispheres flowing toward the low-pressure zone. The rising air at the equator is replaced by poleward-moving air aloft; this air eventually descends around 30° latitude (subtropical high-pressure zones). This describes the overturning Hadley circulation. The ITCZ migrates slightly north/south seasonally following the sun's highest point, but remains near the equator year-round in rainforest regions. This year-round pressure low and rising air create the persistent instability generating daily convectional precipitation.\n\nRELATED CHARACTERISTICS: Humidity is persistently high (80-90% relative humidity) due to abundant moisture from vegetation evapotranspiration and daily precipitation. Dry season is absent; the climate is humid year-round. Wind speeds are generally light because pressure gradients are weak in the equatorial low; the trade winds typical of subtropics don't occur here. Seasonality is minimal; unlike monsoon climates with wet/dry seasons, rainforests have wet year-round. This climatic stability creates ideal conditions for vegetation growth; this explains exceptional biodiversity and high productivity of rainforest ecosystems.",
