        "label": "C",
        "question": "(i) Name one example of an igneous rock and state one location where it can be found; (ii) Describe and explain how igneous rocks are formed.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Granite is an example of an igneous rock found in County Wicklow, where the Wicklow Mountains consist largely of granite granite formed during ancient mountain-building episodes. Granite also occurs in County Donegal and forms part of the Mourne Mountains in Northern Ireland.\n\nIgneous rocks form when molten magma cools and solidifies. Magma originates deep within the Earth's mantle and lower crust where temperatures exceed 700°C. As magma rises through the crust in areas of tectonic activity (particularly at constructive plate margins and hotspots), it enters regions of lower pressure and temperature. The cooling process occurs at different rates depending on depth. When magma cools slowly at depth (hundreds of meters below the surface), it crystallizes slowly, allowing large crystals to form, producing intrusive igneous rocks like granite with visible mineral crystals (quartz, feldspar, mica). When magma erupts at the surface as lava, it cools rapidly, producing fine-grained extrusive rocks like basalt where crystals are too small to see with the naked eye. In Ireland, granite formed as batholiths (large underground chambers of magma) cooled slowly beneath Caledonian mountain ranges. The granite was later exposed at the surface through uplift and erosion of overlying rocks. The cooling magma underwent fractional crystallization where minerals crystallized selectively based on temperature, creating the distinctive mineral composition of granite. Granite's resistance to weathering has preserved these ancient masses as prominent landscape features like mountain ranges where young rocks have eroded away around them.",
