        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Two countries that have joined the European Union since Ireland joined in 1973 are Poland (2004) and Spain (1986). Poland represents the enlargement of the EU into Central/Eastern Europe following the Cold War, while Spain exemplifies expansion within Western Europe.\n\nAdvantages of European Union expansion for Ireland include: First, access to larger markets for Irish goods and services has grown significantly, increasing export opportunities for Irish businesses across expanded EU member states, particularly benefiting agriculture and dairy sectors. Second, EU structural and cohesion funds directed to Ireland as a less wealthy member have funded infrastructure development including roads, telecommunications, and education facilities that enhanced economic competitiveness. Third, expansion created opportunities for Irish workers to migrate and work freely across new member states, facilitating labor mobility and skill transfer. Fourth, Irish companies established operations in new member states to access lower labor costs and new markets, with multinationals using Ireland as a gateway to CEE markets. Fifth, increased EU-wide competition drove Irish firms to innovate and improve efficiency, strengthening their global competitiveness. Sixth, harmonization of standards and regulations across expanded EU facilitated cross-border trade and reduced transaction costs for Irish businesses.\n\nDisadvantages of European Union expansion for Ireland include: First, agricultural competition intensified as new member states like Poland possessed large agricultural sectors, reducing prices received by Irish farmers and necessitating restructuring. Second, structural fund allocation to Ireland has declined as funds shift to newer, less developed member states, reducing investment support for Irish projects. Third, migration from new EU states created labor supply increases that potentially suppressed wages in sectors like hospitality and construction where wages compete with migration-sending countries. Fourth, manufacturing companies relocated to lower-cost new member states, reducing employment in Irish manufacturing sectors. Fifth, expansion increased EU bureaucratic complexity and diluted Ireland's influence in decision-making with more member states requiring consensus. These mixed impacts demonstrate that while EU expansion created significant economic opportunities for Ireland, it also presented competitive challenges requiring economic adaptation.",
