        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Cork city has experienced significant growth as an urban area in Ireland, with its population and economic importance expanding substantially over recent decades. Cork's growth results from multiple interconnected factors.\n\nFactors driving Cork's growth include: First, development as Ireland's second-largest city and designation as a major regional hub has attracted government investment in infrastructure, education, and healthcare facilities that concentrate services and employment in the city. Second, presence of major multinational companies including pharmaceutical firms (Novartis, Pfizer), technology companies (Apple logistics operations), and food processing companies has created substantial employment and attracted skilled workers to relocate to Cork, driving population growth. Third, expansion of Cork Institute of Technology and University College Cork has increased research capacity and graduate production, retaining young educated workers and attracting students from across Ireland and internationally. Fourth, transport improvements including upgraded N/M routes connecting Cork to Dublin and Kerry have reduced commute times and improved accessibility, making Cork more attractive for investment and worker migration. Fifth, cultural amenities including cultural quarter development, festivals (Cork Jazz Festival, Cork Film Festival), and heritage attractions have enhanced Cork's quality of life and attractiveness to young professionals and families. Sixth, development of suburban areas and satellite towns like Mahon and Ballincollig has expanded residential capacity and accommodated population growth fleeing Dublin's high housing costs. Seventh, port development has sustained maritime trade and logistics activities providing employment. Eighth, relatively lower housing costs compared to Dublin initially attracted migrants seeking affordable accommodation while accessing employment opportunities.\n\nThese factors demonstrate that Cork's urban growth reflects economic diversification (multinationals, services), educational expansion, infrastructure improvement, and relative affordability compared to Dublin, making it an increasingly attractive regional center for investment and population in Ireland's economic landscape.",
