        "model": "Dairy farming represents a significant primary economic activity contributing substantially to the economy of the South-West Irish region, particularly in counties Cork, Waterford, and Tipperary. This region has developed Ireland's largest concentration of dairy production due to favorable environmental and economic conditions.\n\nAdvantages for dairy development in the South-West include: First, temperate maritime climate with moderate rainfall supports year-round grass growth essential for pasture-based dairy production, reducing feed costs compared to regions requiring purchased winter fodder. Second, suitable soil types and terrain including limestone-based soils in Tipperary and Waterford support productive grassland and provide natural drainage for livestock farming operations. Third, established dairy processing infrastructure including cooperatives (Dairygold, Glanbia) and creameries concentrates in the region, reducing transportation costs and providing ready markets for milk production. Fourth, proximity to UK and European markets via Cork and Waterford ports facilitates dairy product exports including butter and cheese that generate substantial export revenue. Fifth, availability of agricultural expertise and farming knowledge passed through generations creates skilled labor force and innovation in dairy production techniques. Sixth, agricultural support payments through EU Common Agricultural Policy provide income support stabilizing farm incomes and enabling investment in modern dairy facilities. Seventh, dairy farming integrates with agri-tourism development, farmstays, and cheese-making experiences that diversify farm incomes. Eighth, concentration of dairy farms creates networking opportunities and cooperative advantages for research, marketing, and negotiating input prices. Ninth, expansion opportunities for young farmers through land availability and farm succession in rural areas maintain sector growth.\n\nProblem faced by dairy farming in this region: Intensification of environmental regulations has increased production costs while constraining expansion options. Stringent water quality regulations limiting nitrate and phosphate runoff from concentrated livestock operations necessitate expensive slurry storage facilities and precision nutrient management, raising capital requirements. Climate change impacts including extreme weather events damage grassland productivity and create water stress during dry periods, affecting milk yields. Volatile global milk prices expose farmers to income instability despite production costs remaining relatively fixed, requiring scale increases to maintain viability that create environmental pressures.",
