        "label": "C",
        "question": "(i) Name any one European Union policy which affects Ireland's economy; (ii) Describe and explain the effects of European Union policy(ies) on Ireland's economy.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "(i) The Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) is a European Union policy that significantly affects Ireland's economy.\n\n(ii) The Common Agricultural Policy has profound effects on Ireland's economy, particularly affecting the agricultural sector which remains economically important. The CAP provides direct income support payments to Irish farmers based on land area and production, representing approximately 80% of EU agricultural subsidies; these payments directly support farm incomes and enable farm survival, particularly for smaller and medium-sized holdings that would be uncompetitive without subsidy. The EU milk quotas, which regulated European milk production until 2015, previously restricted Irish dairy production but their abolition has allowed Irish dairy farmers to expand significantly, leading to increased milk production and export of dairy products, strengthening Ireland's position as a major global dairy exporter. CAP regulations also impose production requirements and environmental standards that affect farming practices - farmers must comply with nitrate pollution controls, biodiversity protections, and land management rules, which increase production costs but contribute to environmental sustainability. The CAP's market intervention mechanisms, including price supports and export subsidies, have historically protected Irish agricultural producers from global price competition, though these interventions have been gradually reduced. EU funding for rural development programmes under the CAP supports infrastructure, training, and diversification in rural Irish regions, contributing to economic sustainability beyond farming. CAP regulations and standards have also shaped Ireland's reputation as a producer of high-quality food products meeting rigorous EU standards, creating market differentiation that supports premium pricing for Irish agricultural exports globally. The CAP's trade policy aspects, including negotiating EU trade agreements, affects market access for Irish agricultural products - Irish beef, dairy, and other products benefit from tariff-free access to EU markets and preferential trade arrangements negotiated by the EU. However, CAP policies also present challenges: regulations increase administrative burden and compliance costs for farmers, and EU agricultural decisions are sometimes made by majority vote without full consideration of Irish farming circumstances, potentially imposing policies not optimal for Irish agriculture.",
