    "id": "geography-pp-2024-OR-section3-economicenvironment-Q8",
    "subject": "geography",
    "chapterId": "geo-economic-environment",
    "sectionId": "geo-section3",
    "source": "LC Geography Ordinary 2024 Paper 2 — Q8B",
    "year": 2024,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "B",
        "question": "(i) Name one example of environmental pollution caused by economic activity; (ii) Explain the causes and effects of this environmental pollution; (iii) Describe how this environmental pollution could be reduced.",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "(i) Agricultural nutrient pollution (specifically nitrate contamination of water) is an example of environmental pollution caused by economic activity.\n\n(ii) The causes of agricultural nitrate pollution stem from intensive farming practices and chemical use. The primary cause is excessive application of synthetic chemical fertilizers containing nitrogen compounds to agricultural land; farmers apply these fertilizers to maximize crop and pasture yields, but plants cannot absorb all applied nitrogen, so excess nitrates leach through soil into groundwater and run off into surface water bodies. A second cause is livestock waste management - concentrated animal operations produce large volumes of manure; when stored improperly or spread excessively on land, livestock manure releases nitrates that contaminate water supplies. A third cause involves tillage practices and soil disturbance; ploughing and intensive cultivation accelerate nitrogen mineralization in soils, increasing nitrate leaching. The effects of agricultural nitrate pollution are severe. Nitrate contamination of drinking water sources creates health hazards, particularly for infants and pregnant women, as nitrates can reduce oxygen-carrying capacity of blood. In surface water bodies, excess nitrates cause eutrophication - excessive nutrient loading that stimulates algal blooms and aquatic plant overgrowth. Algal blooms consume dissolved oxygen through respiration, creating anoxic \"dead zones\" where aquatic organisms cannot survive, leading to fish kills and loss of aquatic biodiversity. Nitrate pollution also damages wetland ecosystems and reed beds that depend on nutrient balance. Tourism industries depending on clean water and aquatic recreation suffer economic damage from polluted water bodies.\n\n(iii) Agricultural nitrate pollution could be reduced through several methods. First, precision agriculture techniques using soil testing and targeted fertilizer application reduce excessive nitrogen use - farmers apply only the quantity of fertilizer that plants will actually utilize, minimizing leaching losses. Second, constructing riparian buffer zones and wetlands adjacent to watercourses intercepts nutrient runoff before it reaches water bodies; vegetation in these buffers absorbs and processes excess nutrients through natural biogeochemical cycles. Third, improving livestock waste management through covered storage facilities and controlled land application timing prevents direct manure runoff and allows nutrients to be absorbed by vegetation rather than leaching to groundwater. Fourth, transitioning from intensive conventional agriculture toward organic farming reduces synthetic fertilizer inputs entirely, relying instead on natural nutrient cycling and crop rotation to maintain soil fertility. Fifth, establishing regulations limiting nitrogen application rates per hectare - similar to EU Nitrates Directive requirements - enforces sustainable fertilizer use across regions. Sixth, constructing or improving wastewater treatment facilities removes nitrogen compounds before treated water is discharged to receiving waters. Seventh, restoring natural floodplains allows rivers to overflow and deposit sediments and excess nutrients in nutrient-absorbing wetland vegetation during flood events rather than concentrating pollutants in channels.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 13,
        "scheme_points": 13,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
