    "year": 2024,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "Describe and explain the development of one of the following with reference to a developed economy that you have studied: Tourism; Financial services; Service and footloose industries.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Ireland's tourism sector exemplifies economic development through diversified international visitation and substantial revenue generation. As a developed economy, Ireland has strategically developed tourism, creating thousands of direct and indirect employment opportunities across accommodation, catering, transport and attraction sectors. The country's natural assets—rugged coastlines such as the Cliffs of Moher and Giant's Causeway, paired with cultural heritage including literary associations and ancient historical sites like Newgrange—provide distinctive competitive advantages. Tourism infrastructure development has been systematic: Dublin hosts luxury hotels and conference facilities supporting business tourism; regional airports in Cork, Shannon and Belfast facilitate dispersed visitor arrival; motorway networks enable efficient domestic circulation. Revenue generation demonstrates significance: tourism contributes approximately €6 billion annually to GDP and supports employment in peripheral regions like Galway and Kerry, where limited primary and secondary economic opportunities exist. International marketing campaigns position Ireland as 'the island of saints and scholars' and 'land of 100,000 welcomes,' attracting North American, British and European visitors. The sector's multiplier effect extends development benefits through supply chains—restaurants source from local farmers, transport operators employ locals, accommodation providers purchase linen and food domestically. However, tourism also reflects development challenges: environmental degradation near Skellig Michael and overcrowding in Dublin's Temple Bar demonstrate carrying capacity pressures; dependency on North American and British markets creates economic vulnerability; seasonal employment patterns create underemployment in shoulder months. Tourism integration with other sectors—craft industries selling in duty-free shops, food production marketed internationally—illustrates interconnected economic development. Ireland's tourism development reflects broader EU integration advantages, including visa liberalisation, infrastructure investment funds, and cross-border marketing cooperation, positioning it as Western Europe's accessible cultural destination.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 13,
        "scheme_points": 10,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
