    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "(i) Name one example of how the development of economic activities has resulted in conflict arising between people; (ii) Describe and explain this conflict.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "CONFLICT EXAMPLE: Dam construction in developing economies, such as the Three Gorges Dam in China, exemplifies how economic development creates conflict between people pursuing resource exploitation and those whose livelihoods face destruction.\n\nCONFLICT DESCRIPTION AND EXPLANATION: The Three Gorges Dam development generated significant conflict between multiple stakeholder groups. The Chinese government pursued dam construction for hydroelectric power generation to support industrial development and meet energy demands, reflecting national economic growth priorities. However, the project displaced approximately 1.3 million people from valley settlements, creating immediate conflict over loss of homes, agricultural land, and cultural heritage sites. Fishermen conflicts arose as dam construction altered river hydrology, disrupting spawning grounds and traditional fishing livelihoods accumulated over generations. The reservoir inundation destroyed archaeological sites and submerged villages containing centuries of cultural identity, generating conflict between development advocates prioritizing national energy security and indigenous peoples defending ancestral territories. Agricultural communities faced conflict regarding compensation rates—government resettlement payments proved inadequate compared to productive land productivity losses, creating resentment. Environmental conflict intensified as sedimentation behind the dam altered downstream ecosystems, affecting water quality and navigation in communities dependent on river commerce. Tourism-economy conflicts emerged as scenic gorges flooded, eliminating traditional landscape-based employment. The conflict also reflected philosophical tensions between central-government planning imposing development on local communities without genuine consultation, creating social divisions between migrant workers brought for dam construction and established populations. These conflicts persisted beyond construction completion as promised economic benefits (employment, development investment) proved insufficient for compensating communities for lost resources and cultural destruction, demonstrating how economic development prioritizing aggregate wealth generation frequently generates acute conflicts for localized populations bearing environmental and social costs.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 14,
        "scheme_points": 9,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
