    "sectionId": "geo-section3",
    "source": "LC Geography Ordinary 2024 Paper 2 — Q11A",
    "year": 2024,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "question": "Examine the data above on percentage of population aged over 65 by continent in 2018 and answer each of the following questions: (i) Using graph paper, draw a suitable graph to illustrate this data; (ii) In your answer book, explain briefly one impact of a high percentage of population aged over 65.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "(i) A suitable graph for this data is a bar chart, because the data compares a single value — the percentage of population aged over 65 — across five separate continents.\n\nHow to draw it on graph paper:\n• Title the chart 'Percentage of Population Aged Over 65 by Continent, 2018'.\n• Put the continents — North America, South America, Africa, Europe, Asia — along the horizontal axis and label it 'Continent'.\n• Put the percentage on the vertical axis, labelled 'Percentage of population aged over 65 (%)', scaled from 0 to 20 with a division every 2. The scale must begin at zero and be evenly divided.\n• Draw five bars of equal width, separated by equal gaps, of heights North America 15, South America 8, Africa 3, Europe 18 and Asia 8.\n• Shade the bars, and add a key if they are shaded differently.\n\nMarks are awarded for the title, for labelling both axes, for an accurate and evenly divided scale, for bars of equal width and for plotting each value correctly.\n\n(ii) One impact of a high percentage of population aged over 65: a rising dependency ratio and the cost that comes with it.\n\nThe dependency ratio compares the number of people who are not of working age with the number who are. Where a large share of the population is over 65, there are fewer workers supporting more retired people, so a smaller number of taxpayers must fund the State pension, the health service and long-term care for a larger number of claimants. The demand on health services rises sharply, because older people need more medical care, more prescriptions and, eventually, nursing home places, and demand for home help and community care grows with it.\n\nEurope, at 18%, faces this most acutely: its total fertility rate of about 1.5 children per woman is well below the replacement level of 2.1, so the working-age population is shrinking at the same time as the retired population grows. Governments have responded by raising the pension age, encouraging private pension saving, and using inward migration of working-age adults to fill labour shortages.\n\nThere are positive effects too: older people are increasingly healthy and active, contribute a great deal of unpaid childcare and voluntary work, and form a substantial market — the 'grey economy' — for tourism, leisure and services.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 16,
        "scheme_points": 8,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      }
    ]
