    "source": "LC Geography Ordinary 2024 Paper 2 — Q12C",
    "year": 2024,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "Examine the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map and legend accompanying this paper and answer each of the following questions: (i) Give a grid reference for an area on the map where there is an absence of settlement; (ii) Explain in detail, using evidence from the map, why there is an absence of settlement at this location; (iii) Give a grid reference for an area on the map which has a relatively high density of settlement; (iv) Explain in detail, using evidence from the map, why there is a relatively high density of settlement at this location.",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "Answer with reference to the 1:50 000 Ordnance Survey map of Bantry issued with this paper. Both grid references must be read from the map sheet itself: eastings along the bottom first, estimating tenths across, then northings up the side, estimating tenths up.\n\n(i) An area with an absence of settlement. Look for a grid square in which the map shows no black building symbols at all — on this map that will be on the high ground of the mountains behind Bantry, or on an area of blanket bog, marsh or coniferous plantation.\n\n(ii) Why there is an absence of settlement there.\n\nRelief. The contour lines in such an area are packed closely together and the land rises above 300 or 400 metres. Steep slopes make building difficult and expensive, machinery cannot be used on them, and access roads would have to be cut into the hillside — which is why the map shows no roads there, only occasional tracks.\n\nSoils and drainage. The map shows marsh symbols and the tint of blanket bog across much of this ground. Peat and thin upland soils are acidic, waterlogged and agriculturally worthless, so there is no farming to support a population, and foundations on peat are difficult to build.\n\nClimate and exposure. High ground on an Atlantic coast is colder, far wetter and heavily exposed to the prevailing south-westerly winds, so crops will not ripen and living conditions are harsh.\n\nLand use. Where the map shows coniferous plantation, the land has been given to forestry precisely because it is too poor for anything else, and forestry employs almost nobody.\n\nAccessibility. With no roads, no electricity line and no water supply shown, the area is cut off from schools, shops and employment.\n\n(iii) An area with a relatively high density of settlement. Look for a grid square in which the map shows dense grey built-up shading and a close street network — the town of Bantry itself — or one in which many individual black building symbols line the roads on the low coastal ground.\n\n(iv) Why there is a relatively high density of settlement there.\n\nRelief and site. The contours here are widely spaced and the land lies low, so it is flat, well drained and easy to build on. The town's site at the head of a sheltered bay gave it a harbour, which is why a settlement grew there at all.\n\nTransport. The map shows the N71, the R584 and several local roads converging on the town. Accessibility of this kind attracts people, employment and services, and settlement follows the routeways — which is why the map also shows lines of dwellings strung out along the roads leading out of the town.\n\nEmployment and services. The built-up shading, with symbols for churches, schools, a Garda station, a hospital and a post office, shows a settlement large enough to supply work and services both to its own people and to the surrounding countryside.\n\nSoils and farming. The low coastal ground carries farmland rather than bog, so it supports a farming population as well as feeding the town.\n\nCoastal position. The harbour, piers and the bay itself support fishing, aquaculture, port activity and tourism, all of which sustain population.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 17,
        "scheme_points": 12,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      }
    ]
