        "model": "The Wicklow Mountains in south-central Ireland were formed as part of the Armorican folding (also called Variscan folding) which occurred approximately 300 million years ago during the Carboniferous period. These mountains represent the northwestern extension of a much larger fold mountain belt that stretches across Western Europe from southwest England through Brittany in France. The Wicklow Mountains demonstrate folding processes where rock layers were compressed and uplifted during continental collision.",
