        "label": "A(vii)",
        "question": "State what is meant by the term permeable rock.",
        "marks": 3,
        "model": "Permeable rock is rock containing pore spaces (pores between mineral grains) allowing water to pass through it. Water moves through the pores under gravity or pressure, filtering into the rock mass. Permeable rocks include sandstone (relatively coarse-grained allowing water flow), limestone (containing joints and cavities enabling water movement), and chalk. Permeability differs from porosity: porosity measures pore space percentage; permeability measures ease of water flow through interconnected pores. A rock may be porous (containing pores) but impermeable if pores don't connect. Permeable rock allows groundwater infiltration and movement, creating aquifers storing water supplies used for wells and springs. In karst landscapes, high permeability in limestone enables water penetration and dissolution creating cave systems.",
        "diagram": "",
