      {
        "label": "B",
        "question": "Describe and explain how isostasy has shaped the Irish landscape.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Isostasy—the balance between lithospheric plates and asthenosphere—has profoundly shaped Ireland's landscape. During glaciation (Pleistocene, ending ~10,000 years ago), thick ice sheets (up to 1km thick) depressed Ireland's crust under their massive weight through isostatic compression; the continental crust was pushed down into the asthenosphere. Upon deglaciation as ice melted, this weight was removed and the crust rebounded (isostatic recovery) driving the land upward. This post-glacial isostatic rebound continues today: northeastern Ireland (heavily glaciated) rebounds faster (~0.5mm/year), elevating landscapes relative to sea level. Evidence of isostatic rebound includes: (1) Raised beaches—former shorelines now elevated above current sea level, particularly along northern Ireland coasts where marine deposits occur 10+ meters above current high tide; these beaches formed when glacial meltwater raised sea levels but land subsequently rebounded higher than sea level rise. (2) Coastal features at different elevations—cliff lines and wave-cut platforms elevated above current wave action indicate land emergence from sea level; Lough Neagh and other lakes occur on depressed regions rebounding slower. (3) River rejuvenation—streams cutting new valleys through previously graded floodplains as land uplift increases gradients; this creates knickpoints and waterfalls as streams respond to new base levels. Ongoing rebound tilts Ireland: northwestern regions (glaciated heavily) experience more uplift; southeastern regions subside creating relative sea level rise. This tectonic adjustment of ~0.5mm annually reshapes coastlines, influences river patterns, and determines future landscape evolution. Isostasy explains why Ireland's topography doesn't reflect simple erosional processes but rather combines glacial erosion with post-glacial rebound, creating the distinctive mountainous north and depressed south.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 6,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "Humans interact with the rock cycle in a number of ways. Describe and explain how this interaction with the rock cycle takes place, with reference to one of the following: Mining, Extracting building materials, Oil/gas exploitation, Geothermal energy production.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Humans interact with the rock cycle fundamentally through mining operations. Mining extracts valuable minerals and ores for industrial use (iron, copper, gold) creating massive landscape changes. Mining processes: (1) Surface mining—removing overlying rock (overburden) exposing ore bodies; creates open pit mines with exposed rock faces, waste rock heaps (tailings), and excavated holes. (2) Underground mining—extracting ore at depth through shafts and tunnels. (3) Processing—crushing extracted ore, chemically processing to isolate desired minerals, creating mining waste. Environmental impacts: (1) Landscape disruption—visible scarring from pit mines and tailings; habitat destruction from vegetation removal; elevation changes altering drainage patterns. (2) Water pollution—mining waste (tailings) containing toxic chemicals (cyanide, sulfides) contaminates groundwater and surface water; acidic mine drainage from exposed pyrite oxidation acidifies waterways. Examples: Copper mines in Peru generate massive tailings ponds containing toxic materials; iron mining in Brazil creates pit lakes and water pollution affecting Amazon tributaries. (3) Air pollution—dust from mining operations; processing releases toxic fumes. (4) Economic benefits—mining provides employment and revenue extracting geological resources driving economic development in resource-rich regions (Australia, Chile, Zambia). Rock cycle interaction: mining accelerates rock cycle by extracting rock faster than natural formation; depletes geological resources; processes ore through heating and chemical treatment converting rock types; generates secondary minerals and waste products disrupting natural cycles. Long-term landscape changes: abandoned mines leave permanent scarring; restored mines may create artificial lakes or wildlife habitat. Mining represents one of humanity's most direct interactions with Earth's geology, extracting accumulated geological resources in human timescales rather than geological timescales.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 6,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
