        "label": "C",
        "question": "Examine the factors that have influenced the development of one urban area in an Irish region that you have studied.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Cork city (Ireland's second-largest city) development reflects multiple geographic and socioeconomic factors. Site and situation: Cork's location on the River Lee in South Munster provided natural harbor access—the natural harbor was critical to development enabling trade and food export (particularly butter and beef). Elevation on higher ground provided defensible position and drainage controlling flooding. River crossing point became trading node. Historical development: Cork developed as Norman settlement controlling south coast; medieval walls enclosed settlement; quays developed along river enabling maritime trade. 18th-19th centuries saw expansion as butter trade centered on Cork—creameries and meat-packing plants attracted workers creating urban growth. Geographic factors enabled agricultural hinterland to supply export industries: South Munster's pastoral farming (cattle, dairy) provided raw materials; Cork's harbor enabled export to British and international markets. Transport connectivity: development of roads and railways (Cork-Dublin railway 1859) connected Cork to national market and Dublin; Cork airport provides international connectivity. Modern factors: multinational investments in pharmaceuticals and electronics (Pfizer, EMC) attracted by infrastructure, educated workforce, location within EU market, and government incentives (tax reductions, grants). Services sector growth (universities, hospitals, finance) driving urban expansion. Constraints: distance from Dublin limits economic dominance; peripheral location in Ireland; dependence on external investment rather than indigenous industries. Future factors: sustainable development initiatives, tourism attraction from coastal scenery and historical features, university expansion driving knowledge economy.",
