        "question": "Examine how globalisation impacts on world trade with reference to the operations of one multinational company that you have studied.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Globalisation impacts on world trade significantly through the operations of multinational companies, exemplified by Apple Inc., one of the world's leading technology multinationals. Apple's global operations demonstrate how globalisation reshapes international trade patterns. Apple designs products in the United States but outsources manufacturing to multiple countries, exemplifying global value chain fragmentation. Manufacturing facilities are located in China and Taiwan where labor costs are lower and manufacturing expertise is concentrated, with companies like Foxconn producing iPhone components and assembled devices. This geographic fragmentation reduces Apple's production costs substantially, making products more price-competitive globally and enabling higher profit margins. Raw materials for Apple products are sourced globally: rare earth minerals from China, lithium from Chile and Australia, cobalt from Democratic Republic of Congo, and other components from numerous suppliers worldwide. This global sourcing strategy reduces reliance on single suppliers and manages input costs through global procurement. Distribution of Apple products is global, with products manufactured in Asia exported to markets worldwide including Europe, North America, and emerging markets. Apple operates retail stores in major cities globally, establishing direct consumer contact and brand presence. The company's supply chain involves complex international logistics, with components shipped between countries multiple times before final assembly and distribution to end markets. Globalisation enables this efficiency through reduced trade barriers, international shipping infrastructure, and trade agreements like USMCA and EU regulations. Apple's operations create international trade in components, finished goods, and services. Developing countries like China benefit from manufacturing employment and technology transfer, though critics note exploitative labor conditions in some facilities. Developed countries like the US benefit from high-value design, software development, and service activities, while also experiencing job losses in manufacturing sectors. Apple's global supply chain demonstrates how multinational companies create complex international trade flows, with intermediate goods crossing borders multiple times, fundamentally altering traditional trade patterns where countries exchanged finished products. The company's operations show globalisation concentrating high-value activities (design, marketing, finance) in developed countries while dispersing manufacturing to low-cost locations, creating international inequality in value distribution within global supply chains.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 11,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
