        "model": "A suitable graph to illustrate the Eurozone government debt per capita data would be a vertical column (bar) chart with country names on the x-axis and government debt per person (in € thousands) on the y-axis. The y-axis scale should range from 0 to at least 45 thousand euros, marked at intervals (e.g., 0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45). Seven columns would be drawn, one for each country, with heights corresponding to their debt per capita: Ireland at 43, Greece at 30, Spain at 25, Italy at 38, Belgium at 40, and a horizontal reference line at 29 for the Eurozone Average. The columns should be uniformly spaced and clearly labeled. This column chart format effectively allows visual comparison of relative debt levels across countries and highlights that Ireland (43), Belgium (40), and Italy (38) exceed the Eurozone average (29), whilst Greece (30), Spain (25), and Portugal below the average. The graph should be drawn on graph paper with accurate scaling and clear labeling of axes and title such as 'Eurozone Government Debt Per Capita, 2019'.",
