        "label": "A(iv)",
        "question": "Explain briefly one reason why dependency ratios in urban areas are usually lower than in rural areas.",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "One reason why dependency ratios in urban areas are usually lower than in rural areas is that urban areas attract economically productive working-age migrants. Cities and large towns offer greater employment opportunities, higher wages, better service provision, and superior educational facilities, drawing young adults from rural regions. This selective in-migration of working-age population increases the proportion of economically productive individuals relative to dependents (children and elderly) in urban areas. In contrast, rural areas experience out-migration of working-age people to urban centers, leaving behind older and younger populations, thereby increasing rural dependency ratios.",
