        "question": "Name one form of chemical weathering.",
        "marks": 2,
        "model": "FORM OF CHEMICAL WEATHERING: Oxidation is a form of chemical weathering where rock minerals containing iron react with oxygen, particularly in the presence of water, creating iron oxide (rust). This process weakens rock structure, creating discoloration and eventually breaking rock apart. The rust layer is less dense and strong than original rock, making weathered rock susceptible to mechanical breakdown. Oxidation is particularly prevalent in iron-rich rocks exposed to moist, oxygen-rich environments.",
        "diagram": "",
