        "marks": 4,
        "model": "TWO FACTORS CAUSING ROCK METAMORPHISM: Factor 1: Heat/Temperature increase—rocks deep within Earth's crust experience elevated temperatures from proximity to magma chambers or descending lithosphere in subduction zones, causing mineral recrystallization without melting. Temperatures exceeding 200-300 degrees Celsius initiate metamorphic processes while maintaining solid rock state. Factor 2: Pressure—rocks buried deeply within the crust experience compressive pressure from overlying rock masses, particularly during mountain building and plate collision. Pressure increases with depth (approximately 1 kilobar per 3 kilometers), causing mineral reorientation, recrystallization, and density changes characteristic of metamorphic rocks. Both heat and pressure operate together during tectonic events, transforming parent rocks into metamorphic varieties like slate (from shale) or marble (from limestone).",
