      {
        "label": "1B(i)",
        "question": "Name two examples of sedimentary rocks.",
        "marks": 13,
        "model": "Two examples of sedimentary rocks are sandstone and limestone. Sandstone is formed from compacted and cemented sand grains, typically deposited in shallow marine or desert environments. Limestone is a calcium carbonate rock that forms from the accumulated shells and skeletons of marine organisms, particularly in warm, shallow seas. Both rocks are found extensively throughout Ireland and form a significant part of the rock record visible in cliff exposures and quarries.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 3,
        "scheme_points": 2,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "1B(ii)",
        "question": "State two different Irish locations where sedimentary rocks are found.",
        "marks": 14,
        "model": "Two Irish locations where sedimentary rocks are found are the Burren region in County Clare and the cliffs of Moher, also in County Clare. The Burren is predominantly composed of limestone, creating distinctive karst landscapes with limestone pavements and underground cave systems. The Cliffs of Moher display layered sedimentary rocks, particularly sandstone and shale, which are visible in the dramatic cliff faces reaching up to 200 metres. Both locations provide excellent examples of where sedimentary rocks form prominent landscape features in Ireland.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 3,
        "scheme_points": 2,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "1B(iii)",
        "question": "Describe and explain how sedimentary rocks are formed.",
        "marks": 13,
        "model": "Sedimentary rocks form through a complex process of erosion, transport, deposition, and lithification. Initially, existing rocks on the Earth's surface are broken down by weathering and erosion processes, which physically and chemically fragment rocks into smaller particles such as sand, silt, and clay. These particles are then transported by rivers, wind, or ice to areas of deposition, typically in low-energy environments like riverflood plains, deltas, or shallow seas. Over time, layer upon layer of sediment accumulates in these depositional basins, increasing pressure on the lower layers. The weight of overlying sediment causes compaction, squeezing water out of the sediment and binding particles more tightly together. Simultaneously, chemical processes occur as minerals dissolved in water percolate through the sediment. These dissolved minerals precipitate and cement particles together in a process called cementation, forming solid rock. Different sediments produce different sedimentary rocks: sand becomes sandstone, clay becomes shale, and shell fragments become limestone. The entire process from erosion through to fully lithified rock can take millions of years. Examples include the sandstone quarried in Wicklow and the limestone formations of the Burren. Sedimentary rocks often contain fossils, preserving evidence of past life and environmental conditions, making them invaluable for understanding Earth's history.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 3,
        "scheme_points": 9,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "1C",
        "question": "With the aid of a diagram(s), describe and explain how fold mountains are formed.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Fold mountains form where two tectonic plates converge and push rocks upward into folded structures. A diagram showing fold mountain formation should illustrate: compressional forces acting horizontally from opposite directions; sedimentary rock layers being compressed and pushed upward; anticlinal folds (upfolds) and synclinal folds (downfolds) forming alternating ridge and valley patterns; and the ultimate result of high mountain ranges. The formation process begins when two continental plates collide convergently. The immense compressional forces cause rock layers to buckle and fold rather than break. Sedimentary rocks, which were originally deposited horizontally in ancient seas, become subjected to intense pressure from above and lateral compression from the converging plates. This causes the rock strata to fold into a series of anticlines and synclines. As folding continues, the rock layers are pushed progressively higher, with some rocks reaching considerable altitude. The Alps represent a classic example of fold mountains, formed by the collision of the African plate with the European plate. In Ireland, the Macgillycuddy Reeks and other mountain ranges in County Kerry show folded rock structures. Fold mountains typically occur at destructive plate boundaries where oceanic or continental plates converge. The process is extremely slow, taking millions of years, but the immense pressure involved can cause metamorphic changes in rocks subjected to such intense heat and pressure at depth. Uplift continues as erosion removes material from the mountain tops, allowing isostatic rebound and continued elevation of the folded strata.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 3,
        "scheme_points": 8,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
