        "label": "6A(v)",
        "question": "Explain briefly two reasons why some countries have higher life expectancies than other countries.",
        "marks": 6,
        "model": "Two reasons why some countries have higher life expectancies:\n\n**Reason 1 - Healthcare Access and Quality**: Countries with developed healthcare systems (Japan 84 years life expectancy, Switzerland 83 years) have universal access to preventive care, vaccinations, antibiotics, and treatment for chronic diseases. Mortality reduction from infectious diseases enables longer lifespans. Healthcare investment reduces infant/child mortality (which dramatically reduces average life expectancy when high). Countries with limited healthcare (sub-Saharan Africa, South Asia) experience life expectancies of 50-60 years due to preventable disease deaths and limited access to treatment.\n\n**Reason 2 - Economic Development and Living Standards**: Wealthy countries provide nutrition, clean water, sanitation, and safe housing—fundamental determinants of health. Japan's high life expectancy reflects high income enabling quality nutrition and healthcare access. Conversely, developing countries with poverty, malnutrition, and unsafe water experience shorter lifespans. Economic development enables healthcare investment, while poverty perpetuates poor health outcomes.",
