        "label": "8C",
        "question": "Explain how being a member of the European Union has impacted on the Irish economy.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "EU membership has significantly impacted Ireland's economy through multiple mechanisms:\n\nTrade Integration: EU membership eliminated tariff barriers and customs procedures, enabling Irish exports to access continental markets freely. Agricultural exports particularly benefited from Common Agricultural Policy support. Irish GDP growth accelerated post-EU accession (1973), with living standards converging toward Western European levels.\n\nInvestment Attraction: EU membership status and single market access attracted massive foreign direct investment (FDI) from multinational corporations seeking EU market access. Technology companies (Google, Apple, Facebook, Microsoft) established European headquarters and operations in Ireland, attracted by low corporate tax rates (12.5%) and English language workforce. This FDI created high-value employment and transformed Ireland's economic structure toward services and technology sectors.\n\nStructural Funding: EU structural and cohesion funds financed infrastructure development including motorways, broadband networks, and educational institutions. These investments enhanced Ireland's competitive capacity and productivity.\n\nLabor Migration: EU freedom of movement enabled Irish workers to migrate to prosperous economies seeking employment (reversing historical brain drain), while simultaneously enabling EU workers to migrate to Ireland during labor shortages, addressing workforce constraints.",
