        "model": "Landform formation exemplified by two volcanic features: Cinder cones form from explosive volcanic eruptions ejecting cinder and ash that accumulate around volcanic vents, creating steep-sided conical mountains. Mount Vesuvius shows this structure. Lava flows occur at shield volcanoes where low-viscosity basaltic lava flows easily downslope from volcanic vents, creating gently-sloped mountains and extensive lava fields. Hawaiian volcanoes exemplify shield volcanoes. Alternatively, soil creep represents mass movement where weathered soil particles gradually slide downslope under gravity. Freeze-thaw weathering at high altitudes creates loose rock fragments that creep downslope producing gradual slope lowering. Scree slopes show this process. Slumping involves rapid soil movement along a curved slip plane, creating distinctive curved scars in hillsides. These processes transform landscapes through continuous downslope material movement.",
