        "model": "**Historic Settlements on Ordnance Survey Map**: Three historic Irish settlements typically identifiable on OS maps: (1) Castle/fortified settlement—grid reference (e.g., W250667): Medieval fortified site marked as castle ruin or historic fortification symbol. Description: Built during medieval period for defence and control; narrow streets radiate from central fortress; originally served military and administrative functions. Located at river crossing providing transport and power; defensible position on elevated ground overlooking approaches. Stone construction using local materials. (2) Monastic settlement—grid reference (e.g., M425450): Identified by church/chapel symbol and cross-related placename. Description: Founded by monks for religious community; centrally organized around abbey ruins; contained church, residential quarters, scriptoriums. Located away from main settlements in rural areas; attracted pilgrimage and trade. Often positioned near water for ritual purification and power. (3) Market town—grid reference (e.g., W300680): Identified by street pattern converging at market square; often at road junctions and river crossings. Description: Developed from Norman settlement or medieval grant; regular street pattern reflects planned settlement; central market square indicates commercial function. Located at transport node (river crossing, road junction); attracted merchants and traders; grew through commerce and administration. Different from castle or monastic settlements through commercial focus and planned layout.",
        "diagram": "",
