      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "(i) Name one city where urban sprawl has occurred.\n(ii) Explain the reasons why urban sprawl has occurred in this city.\n(iii) Describe one solution to urban sprawl.",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "Dublin exemplifies urban sprawl in Ireland. Urban sprawl—uncontrolled outward expansion of built-up areas—has characterized Dublin's development. Reasons for sprawl: (1) Economic development and affluence increased housing demand; middle-class residents sought suburban homes with gardens, moving beyond Dublin core to Swords, Tallaght, Blanchardstown. (2) Car ownership expansion (1990s-2008) enabled commuting from distant suburbs to central employment; motorway development (M1, M4, M6) connected suburbs, making longer commutes feasible. (3) Planning policies and land availability: greenfield land availability on urban periphery was cheaper than redevelopment; zoning permitted suburban residential development; developers prioritized low-density suburbs. (4) House prices: central Dublin property prices soared, pushing families to cheaper suburban locations; developers built volume housing estates in periphery. (5) Poor public transport: lack of rapid transit to periphery forced car dependency; people lived far from employment. These factors combined created sprawl extending Dublin from city boundaries into counties Meath, Kildare, Wicklow. Sprawl created urban problems: traffic congestion on limited radial routes; environmental degradation through habitat loss; infrastructure strain (water, electricity); social segregation; agricultural land loss; commuting costs and time burdens. One solution—brownfield regeneration: Redeveloping abandoned industrial and commercial sites within existing urban boundaries. Dublin Docklands redevelopment exemplifies this: former industrial waterfront (17 sq km) was regenerated into mixed-use development with offices, apartments, restaurants. This avoided sprawl, revitalized underused urban land, located development near transport, reduced greenfield pressure. Benefits: compact urban form, reduced commuting distances, infrastructure efficiency, environmental protection. Brownfield regeneration concentrates development inward, constraining sprawl.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 24,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
