        "question": "(i) How many migrants were rescued in May 2015 and how many migrants were rescued in May 2016?\n(ii) Calculate the total number of migrants rescued in 2016 in the three months of May, June and July combined.\n(iii) Name two pull factors that result in people migrating to Europe.\n(iv) Explain briefly one of the pull factors named in part (iii) above.\n(v) Explain briefly one positive impact for the European Union of receiving these migrants.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Based on Mediterranean migration crisis data: (1) May 2015: 501 migrants rescued; May 2016: 522 migrants rescued (slight increase). (2) Total migrants rescued in May-July 2016: 1,334 migrants combined. (3) Two pull factors for European migration: Employment opportunity and economic prosperity—Europe offers formal employment at higher wages than migrants' home countries. EU labour shortages in healthcare, construction, agriculture attract migrants; formal economies provide better income than developing economies. Second factor—better services and living standards—Europe offers superior healthcare, education, social safety nets, and infrastructure compared to conflict-affected or underdeveloped home countries. (4) Explaining employment opportunity: Migrants perceive Europe as having job availability and higher wages. Syria's conflict eliminated employment; European economies with labour shortages recruit migrants. Wage differentials are substantial: Syrian refugee earning €400 monthly in refugee camp versus potential €1200-1500 monthly in Europe. This economic pull motivates dangerous sea crossings. (5) Positive impact for EU: Immigration meets labour needs in healthcare and construction. European ageing populations require care workers; migration provides medical professionals and care workers for expanding elderly care sector. Simultaneously, immigration contributes economic productivity and tax revenue. Young migrant populations offset ageing demographics, sustaining pension systems through increased working-age population contributing taxes. Migrants often work harder and accept jobs locals refuse, increasing overall economic productivity and competitiveness.",
