        "marks": 30,
        "model": "**Macroom's Location Development Using OS Map Evidence**: Three reasons with map evidence: (1) River crossing—Map evidence: River Lee flows through Macroom (identifiable by water feature symbol, blue line). Evidence mark. Explanation: Rivers provided water supply essential for drinking, agriculture, and industry. Rivers also offered transport for trade goods before modern roads. Crossing points became natural settlement locations—bridges facilitated trade and defence. Macroom developed where river could be bridged with established ford, creating commerce hub. (2) Road junction and transport hub—Map evidence: N22 national road and R584 regional road converge at Macroom (identifiable by road symbols and junction pattern). Evidence mark. Explanation: Road junctions became stopping points for traders and travellers; inns and markets developed at junctions. Transport accessibility attracted commerce and settlement growth. Macroom's position at road crossroads made it natural market town. Road connectivity enabled residents to access surrounding rural areas. (3) Defensible position and elevation—Map evidence: Contour lines show terrain; Macroom is positioned on slightly elevated ground relative to surrounding flood plain (note contour spacing and elevation patterns). Evidence mark. Explanation: Elevated positions provided defence against attack (visibility of approaching forces) and flooding (river flood risk was reduced on higher ground). Medieval settlements required defensive advantages. Macroom's elevation combined with river provided both water access and defensive position—optimal for medieval town development. Combination of these three factors—water access, transport accessibility, and defensible position—made Macroom ideal settlement location attracting development over centuries.",
