        "label": "C",
        "question": "(i) Name one city in the developing world that is rapidly growing.\n(ii) Explain the reasons for the rapid growth of this developing world city.\n(iii) Describe the problems caused by this rapid growth.\n(iv) Explain one solution to these problems.",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "Lagos, Nigeria exemplifies rapidly growing developing world city. (1) Reasons for rapid growth: Rural-urban migration driven by rural poverty, agricultural decline, and urban employment perception. Lagos offers manufacturing, port activities, finance, retail employment. Young people migrate seeking opportunity, overwhelming city growth (Lagos grew from 2.4 million in 1990 to 15+ million in 2020). Natural increase—migrants are young; high fertility (4-5 children per woman) produces rapid births exceeding deaths. Colonial legacy of port city creates concentrated development; transport connectivity made Lagos central hub. Oil wealth enabled capital investment and infrastructure development, attracting investment and labour. (2) Problems from rapid growth: Housing shortage creates sprawling slums (Makoko, Shack areas) with inadequate shelter and services. Estimated 70% live in slums lacking sanitation, electricity, clean water. Traffic congestion is severe; inadequate transport infrastructure creates hours-long commutes. Unemployment is high; formal jobs insufficient for population; youth unemployment exceeds 40%. Pollution severe: air quality ranks among world's worst due to vehicles, generators, industry; water pollution from industrial waste and sewage contaminates lagoon and groundwater. Infrastructure strain: water supply inadequate; blackouts frequent due to power shortages; healthcare overwhelmed; schools overcrowded. Insecurity increases: gang violence, kidnapping, armed robbery common in poorer areas; informal governance in slums. Social problems: poverty concentrates; inequality widens; child labour and trafficking occur. (3) Solution—mass transit development: Construction of Bus Rapid Transit (BRT) system provides affordable public transport, reducing traffic congestion and pollution. Dedicated bus lanes and fast service enable commuting from periphery to employment centres. Reduces private vehicle dependency, lowering emissions and congestion. Frees resources for walking and cycling. Enables policing and formal governance along transit corridors. Creates jobs in construction and operations. BRT requires large capital investment but reduces long-term congestion costs and improves urban livability.",
