        "marks": 40,
        "model": "Using West Africa (Nigeria) as the continental region studied:\n\n1. Factors Influencing Agriculture:\nAgriculture in West Africa is significantly influenced by climate variability and rainfall patterns. The region experiences two rainy seasons in equatorial zones and one rainy season in Sahel regions, creating uncertainty for rainfed farming. The harmattan wind in the Sahel brings dry conditions that constrain agricultural productivity. Soil types vary from laterite soils in tropical areas to sandy soils in desert margins, affecting crop suitability and fertility. Topography influences water availability, with river valleys (Niger River) providing fertile alluvial soils suitable for irrigation agriculture. Temperature remains consistently warm year-round, eliminating frost constraints but creating heat stress in some crops. Traditional farming practices and crop varieties adapted over centuries suit local conditions. Population pressure in coastal regions provides labour for agriculture but also drives land degradation through overuse.\n\n2. The Importance of Manufacturing:\nManufacturing provides essential economic diversification for West African regions dependent historically on agriculture. Petroleum refining in Lagos, Nigeria represents the most significant manufacturing industry, processing crude oil for domestic use and export. Food processing (cocoa processing, palm oil refining, textile production) transforms agricultural raw materials into value-added products, increasing income and employment. Metal fabrication, vehicle assembly, and cement production serve growing urban markets. Manufacturing employment provides urban jobs and income, supporting population growth in cities. Export-oriented manufacturing contributes to foreign exchange earnings and government revenue. However, manufacturing remains limited by power supply constraints, limited capital availability, and competition from imports, constraining industrial development compared to developed nations.",
