        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Match each of the letters on the rock cycle diagram (A, B, C) with the stage in the rock cycle most associated with it.\n\nA = Igneous rock (formed from magma cooling at depth or surface)\nB = Sedimentary rock (formed from lithification of sediments)\nC = Metamorphic rock (formed from heat and pressure in deep crust)\n\nThe rock cycle describes the continuous transformation of rock types through geological processes. Igneous rocks form when magma cools and solidifies; if cooling occurs slowly at depth, large crystals form (plutonic rocks like granite); rapid surface cooling creates small crystals (volcanic rocks like basalt). Sedimentary rocks form when weathered rock fragments (sediments) are transported, deposited, compacted, and cemented (lithified). Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks are subjected to intense heat and pressure at depth, causing mineral recrysta",
