        "model": "The four glacial landforms shown in the photographs correspond as follows: Photograph A shows a sea arch, formed by marine erosion where waves exploit weakness in coastal rock strata, creating an arch as the roof remains while the base is widened. Photograph B depicts a corrie or cirque, a bowl-shaped amphitheatre carved by rotational slip and abrasion of a glacier confined within a mountain valley head. Photograph C displays an arête, a sharp-edged ridge formed where two corries erode back-to-back, removing material from both sides. Photograph D shows an interlocking spur, where a river winds through a valley and erosion leaves alternating hillsides that appear to interlock when viewed along the valley, a feature common in youthful fluvial landscapes.",
