    "id": "geography-pp-2018-OR-section2-economicactivities-Q5A",
    "subject": "geography",
    "chapterId": "geo-economic-activities",
    "sectionId": "geo-section2",
    "source": "LC Geography Ordinary 2018 Part Two — Q5A",
    "year": 2018,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "(i)",
        "question": "The greatest value of Irish fish was exported to which region in 2016?",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "The European Union.\n\nThe bar chart shows Irish fish exports to the EU at close to €430 million in 2016, far above any other region.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 20,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(ii)",
        "question": "Explain briefly one reason why the greatest value of Irish fish was exported to this region.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "The European Union takes the greatest value of Irish fish exports because Ireland has tariff-free access to it and it is close by.\n\nAs a member of the EU, Ireland can sell into the Single Market of over 440 million consumers without tariffs or customs delays. That matters enormously for fish, which is highly perishable: a lorry-load landed at an Irish port can be on the market in France, Spain or Italy within a day or two, and speed is what preserves both the quality and the price. The main European markets — France, Spain and Italy — also have a strong tradition of eating fish and shellfish, so demand for Irish salmon, mackerel, prawns and mussels is high and consumers there are willing to pay well for them. Shipping the same product to Asia or Africa takes far longer and costs far more.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 20,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(iii)",
        "question": "The value of Irish fish exports to which region underwent the greatest percentage increase between 2015 and 2016?",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "EU membership has profoundly shaped Ireland's economic development since accession in 1973. Initially, Ireland benefited enormously from Agricultural Structural Funds and Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) supports, dramatically raising rural incomes and enabling agricultural modernization. Subsequently, Structural Funds and European Regional Development Funds financed infrastructure development (roads, telecommunications, education), creating foundations for economic growth. Ireland attracted multinational corporations seeking EU market access: pharmaceutical companies (Pfizer in Cork, Merck in Tipperary), technology companies (Google, Apple, Microsoft establishing European headquarters), and financial services institutions concentrated in Dublin. EU investment in education and training programs enhanced human capital. Trade integration increased prosperity as Irish exports reached European markets duty-free. Ireland's corporation tax rate (12.5%), among Europe's lowest, further attracted foreign direct investment. Consequently, Ireland transformed from a peripheral, economically struggling country in 1973 to a prosperous 'Celtic Tiger' by the 1990s with contemporary GDP per capita among Europe's highest.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 20,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "(iv)",
        "question": "Calculate the difference (in millions of euro), between the value of fish exports to the European Union and the value of fish exports to Nigeria & North Africa in 2015.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "€300 million.\n\nReading the 2015 figures from the bar chart: exports to the European Union were about €400 million and exports to Nigeria & North Africa about €100 million, so the difference is 400 − 100 = €300 million.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 20,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "(v)",
        "question": "Explain briefly one advantage of exports to the Irish economy.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "The Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) remains the EU's largest budget expenditure (approximately 38% of the EU budget), distributing approximately €60 billion annually to member states' agricultural sectors. CAP provides income support to farmers through direct payments based on land area farmed, market intervention purchasing surplus production to maintain prices, and rural development funding for infrastructure and training. Historically, CAP focused on maximizing production through price support, encouraging intensive farming and overproduction. Recent CAP reforms (2013 onwards) introduce environmental requirements: farmers must implement greening measures (crop diversification, ecological focus areas, permanent grassland preservation) to receive full payments. Critiques include: support predominantly benefits large landowners rather than small farmers, production quotas and price supports distort global markets disadvantaging developing countries, environmental impacts of intensive agriculture (fertilizer pollution, biodiversity loss) persist despite reforms, and budget costs crowd out alternative EU expenditures. Ireland's farming community depends heavily on CAP payments: approximately 80% of Irish farm income derives from CAP supports, creating vulnerability if support levels decline.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 20,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "(vi)",
        "question": "State two advantages that Ireland has for fishing.",
        "marks": 5,
        "model": "Future challenges for EU economic integration include climate change requiring coordinated carbon reduction targets and green infrastructure investment, digital transformation and technology sector concentration raising inequality concerns, post-pandemic economic recovery requiring massive investment in debt-burdened member states, energy security following Russian invasion of Ukraine disrupting gas supplies and forcing renewable energy acceleration, migration pressures from conflict regions and economic disparities, and political fragmentation with populist movements challenging EU integration in numerous countries. Trade tensions with the USA and China require strategic responses, while internal tensions between wealthy northern and peripheral southern/eastern members persist. Addressing these challenges requires strengthened cooperation, adequate financing mechanisms, and political commitment to maintaining European unity despite divergent national interests.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 20,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
