        "question": "By the year 2031, there will be nearly one million people over the age of 65 in Ireland. That will have been an increase of more than 86% since 2016. Discuss the challenges associated with an ageing population and how this ageing population can be planned for.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Migration to, from, and within Ireland reflects complex economic and social forces. Outward migration has characterized Irish history: 1800s famine emigration sent 1+ million people to USA, Australia, and Canada; late 1800s-early 1900s economic hardship drove continued emigration; 1950s-1960s limited employment caused emigration to UK and USA; 1980s recession drove emigration as unemployment exceeded 15%; and contemporary young Irish professionals emigrate for career opportunities and high wages (Australia, USA, UK popular destinations). Inbound migration: EU membership (1973) gradually facilitated Eastern European migration post-2004, creating immigrant communities in Irish cities; migrant workers supply labour shortages in healthcare (Filipino nurses), construction (Polish workers), and hospitality; and refugees and asylum seekers from conflict regions (Syria, Afghanistan) arrive seeking protection. Internal migration: rural-to-urban migration concentrates population in Dublin (now 1.4+ million metro area) and other cities, driven by employment availability and service access; young people leave rural areas lacking opportunities; and return migration of Irish emigrants from abroad selectively concentrates in Cork, Galway, and coastal areas offering lifestyle quality. Contemporary Ireland experiences net immigration, reversing historical emigration, though emigration continues for specific age groups seeking wages unavailable at home.",
