      {
        "label": "A(v)",
        "question": "Briefly explain two reasons why there was a decrease in the number of fishing vessels in the EU between 2008 and 2017.",
        "marks": 4,
        "model": "Reason 1: European Union fishing quotas and fleet reduction policy. Under the Common Fisheries Policy the EU sets a Total Allowable Catch for each stock each year and divides it between member states as national quotas. Because stocks such as cod, herring and mackerel had been heavily overfished, the quotas were cut repeatedly, and the EU also operated decommissioning schemes that paid owners to scrap vessels in order to bring fleet capacity into line with the fish available. Ireland's own decommissioning schemes removed a substantial part of the whitefish fleet in this way. The table reflects the policy: the total EU fleet fell from 85,441 vessels in 2008 to 82,737 in 2017, with Greece down by over 2,000 and Spain by over 2,200.\n\nReason 2: the rising cost of fishing and falling profitability. Fuel is the single largest running cost of a trawler, and fuel prices rose sharply over the period. At the same time crews became harder to recruit, insurance and safety compliance costs rose, and competition from cheap imported and farmed fish held prices down. Many small operators found that the boat could no longer be run at a profit and either sold their quota to larger operators or left the industry. The result is a smaller number of larger, more efficient vessels — which is why the Netherlands, with a highly capitalised fleet, actually increased its vessel numbers slightly while the traditional small-boat fleets of the Mediterranean shrank.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 7,
        "scheme_points": 4,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "question": "Examine two factors that have influenced the development of tourism in a Continental / Sub-Continental region (not in Europe) that you have studied.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The Continental / Sub-Continental region examined here is the Indian sub-continent, and the two factors are its attractions and government policy.\n\nFactor 1: physical and cultural attractions. Tourism in India rests on a resource base that is exceptional in both range and quality. The built heritage is the principal draw: the Taj Mahal at Agra receives 7–8 million visitors a year, and the temples of Khajuraho, the rock-cut caves at Ajanta and Ellora, Amber Fort at Jaipur and the Golden Temple at Amritsar are internationally known. These are concentrated enough to be packaged: the 'Golden Triangle' of Delhi, Agra and Jaipur can be covered in a short circuit and is the most heavily used tourist route in the country.\n\nThe physical resource is equally varied. The Himalayas in the north offer trekking, mountaineering and the hill stations of Shimla, Manali and Darjeeling, developed originally by the British as an escape from the heat of the plains and now marketed to domestic tourists. Goa's beaches, developed from the 1960s, take over 8 million visitors a year, and Kerala's backwaters and Ayurvedic resorts have created a distinct high-value niche. The national parks — Ranthambore, Kaziranga, Periyar — supply wildlife tourism, including tiger and rhinoceros viewing.\n\nClimate reinforces the attraction: the dry season from October to March is warm and reliable and coincides exactly with the northern European and North American winter, giving India a natural advantage in the long-haul winter-sun market. The monsoon from June to September, by contrast, effectively closes much of the country to tourism, which makes the industry strongly seasonal.\n\nFactor 2: government policy and infrastructure investment. The resource would not have generated the growth it has without deliberate development. The Incredible India campaign, launched in 2002, marketed the whole country as a single brand for the first time and is credited with a substantial part of the rise in foreign arrivals from about 2.5 million in 2000 to roughly 10 million by 2017. The e-visa scheme, extended to more than 160 countries, removed the single greatest practical obstacle to travel. The Ministry of Tourism's Swadesh Darshan scheme funds themed circuits — Buddhist, coastal, Himalayan — and individual states run their own campaigns, of which Kerala's 'God's Own Country' has been the most successful.\n\nTransport investment has been just as important. The liberalisation of Indian aviation created low-cost carriers such as IndiGo and SpiceJet, which have made internal travel cheap enough for a mass domestic market, and new terminals at Delhi and Mumbai have raised international capacity sharply. The railway network, at over 68,000 km, carries the bulk of domestic tourism, and luxury services such as the Palace on Wheels have been developed as attractions in their own right. Foreign investment in hotels by Marriott, Hilton and the Indian Taj group has provided the accommodation.\n\nTourism now contributes close to 9% of Indian GDP and supports roughly 40 million jobs. Growth is constrained, however, by poor sanitation, air pollution in Delhi, concerns about personal safety that damaged the country's image after 2012, and by overcrowding at the major sites — visitor numbers at the Taj Mahal are now capped because pollution and pressure were damaging the marble.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 7,
        "scheme_points": 30,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "Discuss the factors which influenced the development of one urban area in a European region (not in Ireland) that you have studied.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The urban area examined here is Paris, in the Paris Basin region of France.\n\nSite and situation. Paris began as the Roman settlement of Lutetia on the Île de la Cité, an island in the Seine. The site was chosen because the island offered an easily defended position and because it split the river into two narrower channels, making it the easiest crossing point for many kilometres — a classic bridging point. The land around is flat and well drained, so the settlement could expand in every direction without physical obstacle. Its situation is even more important: Paris lies at the centre of the Paris Basin, a saucer-shaped lowland into which the Seine, Marne, Oise and Yonne all converge. Any route across northern France naturally passes through it, so the city became the focus of the country's whole transport network.\n\nPhysical resources. The Seine provided water, a defensive line, a means of transport and, later, a navigable link to the sea at Le Havre. The limestone of the Basin supplied building stone — quarried from beneath the city itself, leaving the catacombs — and the limon soils of the surrounding Beauce and Brie fed the growing population, so the city never outran its food supply.\n\nPolitical function. Paris became the capital of the Capetian kings in the tenth century, and centralisation under successive French governments concentrated administration, the court, the church and the university there. The nineteenth-century rebuilding under Haussmann drove wide boulevards through the medieval city, laid down water and sewerage, and created the street pattern the city still has. As capital it now houses the national government, the head offices of most French companies, and international bodies including UNESCO and the OECD.\n\nTransport. Routes were deliberately built to radiate from Paris, producing a hub-and-spoke pattern across France. The TGV network, begun with the Paris–Lyon line in 1981, now radiates in every direction at 300 km/h; Charles de Gaulle Airport handles over 70 million passengers a year; the Channel Tunnel links the city directly to London; and the Paris Métro, opened in 1900, is one of the largest urban rail systems in the world. This accessibility attracts business and tourism in a self-reinforcing cycle.\n\nEconomic development. Industry located in Paris because of the market, the labour supply and the transport: Renault at Boulogne-Billancourt and later the aerospace and electronics industries. As manufacturing moved out, the city specialised in services — finance at La Défense, which is Europe's largest purpose-built business district, fashion, publishing and research. Tourism is a major industry in its own right: Paris receives around 30 million visitors a year, drawn by the Louvre, the Eiffel Tower, Notre-Dame and Versailles.\n\nPlanning and growth management. Rapid twentieth-century growth was managed by deliberate planning. Five new towns, including Marne-la-Vallée and Cergy-Pontoise, were built from the 1960s to absorb population; the RER regional express network was built to connect them; and La Défense was developed to keep high-rise office building out of the historic centre. The Grand Paris Express, now under construction, is adding 200 km of automated metro to link the suburbs to one another rather than only to the centre.\n\nProblems. Growth has produced severe housing pressure and very high prices in the centre; the post-war banlieues built to house migrant workers have high unemployment and have been the site of repeated social unrest; traffic congestion and air pollution are serious; and the dominance of Paris over the rest of France — a phenomenon the French call the 'désert français' — has drained population and investment from provincial regions.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 7,
        "scheme_points": 30,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      }
