    "sectionId": "geo-section4",
    "source": "LC Geography Higher 2021 Paper 2 — Q14",
    "year": 2021,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "Discuss",
        "question": "Sustainability is a model for future human and economic development. Discuss this statement with reference to three of the following: Resource use, Fair trade, Justice issues, Self-reliance.",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "Sustainable development is development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs — the definition given in the Brundtland Report of 1987. Three aspects are discussed here: resource use, fair trade, and self-reliance.\n\nRESOURCE USE\n\nThe problem. The world's economy is built on resources consumed far faster than they are replaced. Fossil fuels took hundreds of millions of years to form and are being burned in a few centuries; groundwater in the Punjab and the American High Plains is being pumped out faster than it recharges; fish stocks have been reduced to a fraction of their former size; and soil is eroded faster than it forms. Consumption is also grossly unequal: if everyone lived as the average person in Ireland or the United States does, humanity would need several planets. Renewable resources become non-renewable in practice once they are used beyond their rate of replacement.\n\nSustainable practice. Sustainability requires using renewable resources within their replacement rate and substituting renewables for finite ones. In energy this means the transition Ireland has committed to — 51% emissions reduction by 2030, wind already supplying about a third of Irish electricity, the closure of Moneypoint and the end of peat-fired generation. In fisheries it means the quota system of the Common Fisheries Policy, which sets a Total Allowable Catch based on scientific advice on stock size, together with mesh-size rules, closed areas and the landing obligation; stocks such as North Sea cod have begun to recover where quotas have been respected. In forestry it means selective felling and replanting under FSC certification rather than clear-felling. In materials it means the circular economy — designing products to be repaired and recycled rather than discarded, which the EU has legislated for through the Circular Economy Action Plan and the right-to-repair rules.\n\nLimits. Reducing consumption is politically difficult, because economies are structured around growth and because those asked to consume less are often those who have consumed least. Renewable technologies themselves need finite materials — lithium, cobalt, rare earths — whose extraction causes serious environmental and human damage in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and elsewhere. And quotas only work where they can be enforced, which is why illegal fishing remains a major problem in west African waters.\n\nFAIR TRADE\n\nThe problem. The terms on which developing countries trade are systematically unfavourable. They export primary products — coffee, cocoa, cotton, minerals — whose prices are volatile and set on world exchanges they do not control, and import manufactured goods whose prices are stable and rising. Value is added elsewhere: of the retail price of a bar of chocolate, the cocoa farmer receives around 6%. Subsidies in the EU and United States allow their farmers to sell below cost, undercutting producers in poorer countries in their own markets, and tariff escalation — low tariffs on raw cocoa beans, high tariffs on processed chocolate — deliberately prevents developing countries from moving up the value chain. Roughly two million children work in cocoa production in Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana, largely because farm incomes are too low to pay adult labour.\n\nThe fair trade response. Fairtrade certification guarantees producers a minimum price that covers the cost of sustainable production regardless of the world price, plus a social premium paid to the co-operative to spend on schools, clinics or water supply as it chooses. It requires democratic co-operative organisation, bans forced and child labour, and sets environmental standards. Around 1.9 million farmers and workers in over 70 countries are covered, and Ireland has one of the highest rates of Fairtrade product recognition in the world.\n\nHow it supports sustainability. A guaranteed price allows a farmer to plan and to invest — in shade trees, in soil conservation, in replacing ageing cocoa stock — rather than maximising this season's yield at the cost of next decade's. It funds education, which is the single most effective long-term development measure. And it keeps more of the value in the producing country.\n\nLimits. Fairtrade covers only a small share of world trade in the relevant commodities; certification is expensive and tends to favour better-organised co-operatives over the poorest farmers; the premium is small relative to the retail price; and there is a risk that guaranteeing a price encourages overproduction of a crop for which demand is limited. It treats a symptom of an unequal trading system rather than the system itself. Reform of WTO rules, removal of agricultural subsidies and an end to tariff escalation would do more.\n\nSELF-RELIANCE\n\nThe idea. Self-reliance means meeting needs from local resources, using local skills and appropriate technology, so that a community is not dependent on outside aid, on imported inputs it cannot afford, or on prices set elsewhere. It is the opposite of the large, capital-intensive, imported-technology development model that dominated aid policy for decades.\n\nIn practice. Appropriate technology is the core of it: a treadle pump a farmer can maintain rather than a diesel pump requiring imported fuel and spare parts; a solar lantern replacing kerosene; a fuel-efficient stove that cuts firewood use and indoor smoke; rainwater harvesting and stone bunds that a village can build with its own labour. Micro-finance — small loans, often to women, without collateral — allows people to start businesses without depending on outside investors; the Grameen Bank model has been replicated worldwide and has repayment rates above 95%. In agriculture, self-reliance means growing food for local consumption alongside cash crops, saving and exchanging seed rather than buying it annually, and using compost and crop rotation rather than imported fertiliser.\n\nWhy it is sustainable. Locally understood technology is maintained and lasts; imported equipment breaks and is abandoned. Local control means decisions are taken by the people who bear their consequences. Diversified production means a community is not ruined when a single world price collapses. Growing food locally reduces transport emissions and vulnerability to global grain price shocks of the kind seen in 2008 and 2022. And self-reliance builds skills and confidence that persist after any particular project ends.\n\nLimits. Self-reliance alone cannot supply everything a modern economy needs — hospitals, universities, electricity grids and roads require capital on a scale that local savings cannot generate — and it can become an excuse for withholding aid or for accepting a low ceiling on development. It works best in combination with fair access to external markets and with public investment.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThe three aspects reinforce one another. Fair prices give producers the security to farm sustainably rather than to strip their land; self-reliance reduces dependence on resources and prices controlled elsewhere; and sustainable resource use is what allows any of it to continue. The obstacle is not knowledge but distribution of power: those who consume most are not those who bear the cost. Sustainability is therefore a workable model for future development, but only if it is applied to the wealthy economies as well as to the poor ones.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 20,
        "scheme_points": 80,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      }
    ]
