        "label": "Examine",
        "question": "Examine how each of the following activities impact on biomes: The felling of tropical rain forests, Intensive agricultural practices, Industrial development.",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "Human activities significantly impact biomes through the tropical rainforest felling, intensive agriculture, and industrial development. Tropical rainforest felling exemplifies the most dramatic biome destruction. The Amazon rainforest, Earth's largest tropical biome spanning 5.5 million square kilometres, loses approximately 17,000 hectares daily to logging, cattle ranching, and soybean cultivation. Rainforest destruction eliminates habitat for 90% of terrestrial species; species like the jaguar and anaconda face extinction. Deforestation disrupts hydrological cycles: rainforests generate 50% of their own rainfall through transpiration; removal reduces regional precipitation and potentially triggers savannization. Carbon storage collapses—tropical forests store 230 tonnes of carbon per hectare; deforestation releases this as atmospheric CO2, accelerating climate change. Indigenous communities lose traditional territories and medicinal plant sources. Forest succession proceeds toward secondary scrubland or grassland rather than returning to forest without centuries of recovery. Intensive agricultural practices drastically alter biome productivity and structure. Monoculture cultivation (palm oil plantations, wheat fields) replaces biodiverse natural vegetation with single-species crops. These systems require massive pesticide and fertilizer inputs: pesticides kill non-target insects, reducing pollinator populations by 75% in some regions; runoff creates aquatic dead zones. Soil degradation from continuous cultivation reduces fertility and increases erosion rates. In Sub-Saharan Africa, overgrazing of savanna biomes compacts soil, destroys vegetation structure, and triggers desertification, pushing the Sahara southward at 5-10 km annually. Irrigation systems for agriculture deplete aquifers (Ogallala Aquifer beneath US Great Plains faces collapse) and reduce river flows to wetland biomes. Industrial development directly destroys biome structure through infrastructure: mining operations in tropical regions (Bougainville copper mine in Papua New Guinea) create massive ecological footprints, stripping vegetation and contaminating water systems; oil extraction in boreal regions damages permafrost stability and impacts migratory species; power plant construction fragments habitats. Pollution from industrial operations—heavy metals in wetlands, air pollution affecting montane ecosystems—disrupts ecosystem functions. All three activities share common consequences: biodiversity loss, disrupted nutrient cycles, altered energy flows, reduced ecosystem resilience, and climate impacts. The cumulative effect threatens planetary carrying capacity and human welfare depending on biome services (pollination, water purification, climate regulation) valued at $125 trillion annually.",
