        "question": "Examine how people express their culture and identity in everyday life with reference to Irish examples that you have studied.",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "People express culture and identity in everyday life through multiple Irish contexts and practices. Food and cuisine represent primary cultural expression: traditional Irish stew made with lamb, potatoes, and onions connects to rural pastoral heritage; soda bread and brown bread reflect peasant agricultural reliance on wheat and potatoes; seafood consumption in coastal communities (Galway oysters, Dublin coddle) reflects maritime identity. Pub culture embodies Irish social identity—pubs serve as community gathering spaces where storytelling, traditional music sessions (céilí), and whiskey consumption create belonging. The Guinness brand internationally represents Irish identity. Breakfast traditions (full Irish breakfast with black pudding and soda bread) differ from English variants, marking distinct cultural identity. Traditional music and dance constitute central cultural identity expression: bodhran drumming, uilleann pipes, tin whistle melodies reflect Celtic heritage; step dancing competitions like Riverdance showcase Irish identity globally. Traditional set dancing in rural parishes maintains community bonds and cultural continuity across generations. Language use, particularly Irish (Gaeilge), signals cultural commitment: Gaeltacht regions (West Cork, Donegal, Mayo) maintain native Irish speakers; place names (Baile, Dun, Cnoc prefixes) embed Irish language in landscape. Irish language education increasingly signals cultural pride among younger Dublin professionals. Religious practice, particularly Catholic traditions, historically shaped Irish identity: pilgrimage to Knock shrine, St. Brigid's Day celebrations, and Holy Communions remain community rituals. Cross-border identity complexities in Northern Ireland show religion marking political affiliation (Catholic=Irish, Protestant=British). Dress and personal adornment express identity: Aran sweater patterns identify regional origins; Celtic knotwork jewelry signals Irish ethnicity; tricolour clothing worn on St. Patrick's Day broadcasts cultural pride. Sporting identity: Gaelic football and hurling represent uniquely Irish sports connecting to national identity; GAA club membership defines rural community identity; rugby and soccer followings reveal international cultural influences. Architecture and settlement patterns embody identity: Georgian terraces in Dublin's south side signal wealth and cultural status; whitewashed thatched cottages represent rural heritage and tourist attractions; megalithic tombs (Newgrange, Carrowmore) link contemporary Irish identity to ancient Celtic civilizations. Family structures and kinship practices: emphasis on extended family gatherings, emigration patterns that maintain diaspora connections to homeland, baptism and funeral rituals bind communities. Storytelling traditions: sean-nós singing (traditional unaccompanied vocals) and literary traditions (Joyce, Heaney, Doyle) represent Irish cultural expression reaching global audiences. Names themselves express identity: Irish Gaelic names (Siobhán, Pádraig, Deirdre) affirm cultural heritage; anglicized names (Joan for Siobhán) historically represented assimilation pressures. Contemporary Irish identity increasingly balances local/national pride with European integration and global influences, reflecting post-colonial cultural reclamation while embracing multiculturalism.",
