        "question": "Examine the uneven distribution of temperature over the earth's surface.",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "Temperature distributes unevenly across Earth's surface due to differential solar radiation receipt and atmospheric/oceanic circulation patterns. Solar radiation input varies with latitude: equatorial regions receive intense perpendicular radiation year-round; polar regions receive slant radiation over shorter days or winter darkness, receiving <25% of equatorial radiation annually. This latitudinal gradient fundamentally drives global temperature patterns: equator averages 25-28°C; mid-latitudes average 10-15°C; poles average -20 to -40°C. However, actual temperatures deviate substantially from simple latitudinal prediction. Seasonal variation reflects axial tilt: Northern Hemisphere summer (June) receives maximum radiation creating +35°C temperatures in continental interiors (Death Valley 57°C); winter conversely brings -60°C in Siberian permafrost regions. Southern Hemisphere seasons invert. Continentality (distance from ocean) creates extreme temperature ranges: continental interiors (Kansas, central Asia) show 40-50°C annual ranges; coastal regions (San Francisco, Western Ireland) show <15°C ranges due to oceans' thermal inertia. Ocean currents redistribute heat poleward: the Gulf Stream carries warm equatorial water northeast, warming British Isles to 10°C despite 56°N latitude—locations 10° south remain at 0°C. The Kuroshio Current similarly warms Japan. Conversely, cold currents: the Peru Current brings 15°C water to 12°S latitude, creating arid desert conditions; the Benguela Current creates South African aridity. Atmospheric circulation patterns create temperature anomalies: subtropical high-pressure cells (30°N/S) suppress cloud cover and precipitation, creating hot deserts (Sahara, Arabian, Australian); tropical convergence zones bring rising air, latent heat release, and warm wet conditions. Jet stream positions shift seasonally, explaining why North America's Arctic air masses reach 40°S in winter but +35°C summers in the same locations. Mountain topography disrupts temperature patterns through elevation effects: 1°C decrease per 100 metres elevation means alpine regions 4,000m high average 20°C cooler than adjacent lowlands. The Tibetan Plateau creates massive atmospheric heating effects, driving monsoon circulations. Albedo (reflectivity) variations modify local temperatures: Arctic sea ice reflects 90% of radiation, maintaining polar cold; dark forest surfaces absorb 90%, creating local warmth. Anthropogenic greenhouse gases increasingly override natural patterns: CO2 concentrations rising 50% since industrialization create global warming overriding regional circulation patterns. Permafrost regions now thaw; glaciers retreat globally. Urban heat islands—cities 5-10°C warmer than surroundings due to dark surfaces and heat emissions—create local temperature anomalies. Thus, Earth's temperature distribution results from complex interactions between solar input, circulation systems, oceanic heat transport, topography, albedo, and increasingly, human influences.",
