        "model": "Drawing an outline map of Ireland requires careful representation of the country's overall shape and border. The map must show Ireland's key geographical features at appropriate scale, depicting the island's irregular coastline and characteristic dimensions. To show the outline of one named region, identify a core or peripheral Irish region and draw its boundary—examples include the Dublin Region (core eastern region), West Region (peripheral), Southwest Region (peripheral), or Midlands Region (intermediate). The urban centre must be named and accurately located within the chosen region—examples include Dublin City (Dublin Region), Cork City (Southwest), Galway City (West), or Limerick City (Midwest). The river should be correctly positioned within the region's boundaries—examples include the River Liffey (Dublin Region), River Lee (Southwest), River Shannon (West/Midwest), or River Moy (Northwest). The relief feature outline must clearly show a named upland or lowland area within the region—upland examples include the Wicklow Mountains (Dublin Region), Macgillycuddy's Reeks (Southwest), or Connemara Mountains (West); lowland examples include the Midlands Plain or the Dublin Basin. Marks are awarded for correct map outline (4 marks), correct showing of each of four features (2-3 marks each graded 2/1/0), and correct naming of each feature (2 marks each). The map may include a key for naming or names written directly on features. Features must be named correctly to earn full marks—if shown but incorrectly named or not named, 0 marks are awarded for both showing and naming that feature.",
