        "question": "Examine two factors that have influenced the development of one tertiary economic activity in an Irish region that you have studied.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Tourism exemplifies tertiary economic activity development in the Cork/Southwest Irish region, influenced by multiple geographic factors. First, natural attractions and climate influence tourism development significantly. The Southwest coast features dramatic, distinctive landscapes including the Macgillycuddy's Reeks mountains, Ring of Kerry scenic route, and Dingle Peninsula—these natural attractions are primary tourist destinations attracting international visitors. The region's accessibility via coach routes and natural harbours (Kenmare Bay, Dingle Harbour) supported early tourism infrastructure development. Coastal scenery and associated activities (walking, boating, fishing) created economic opportunities for tourism businesses. The climate, while cool and wet, offers dramatic weather patterns and mountain scenery that attract adventure tourists. The Gulf Stream moderates Atlantic exposure, creating mild winters suitable for coastal visitation. Seasonal variation creates concentrated tourist seasons (July-August, Easter, Christmas holidays) that structure tourism employment and revenue patterns. Second, transport connections and historical cultural heritage drive tourism development. The development of the N71 and N70 coastal roads improved accessibility, enabling coach tourism and private vehicle visitors. Cork Airport expansion and ferry services from Cherbourg support tourist arrival routes. Historic sites including Skellig Michael (UNESCO World Heritage Site), Dunloe Castle, ancient monastic sites and medieval towns attract cultural tourists. The region's connection to Irish language and culture (Gaeltacht areas in County Kerry) attracts language learners. Literary heritage (connection to authors like Frank McCourt from Limerick area) enhances cultural tourism appeal. The Ring of Kerry circular route became branded as a tourism product, creating employment in accommodation, hospitality and guiding services. Traditional Irish pub culture and traditional music venues provide experiential attractions. Hotel and restaurant development concentrated along the Ring of Kerry demonstrates how tourism infrastructure clusters where natural attractions and heritage create draw. Third, EU funding and government tourism promotion policies have accelerated development. European Regional Development Fund grants supported infrastructure including roads, harbours and visitor facilities in less-developed Southwest region. Irish government tourism marketing promoted the Southwest internationally, particularly targeting North American and European markets. The designation of Skellig Michael as a World Heritage Site brought international recognition and visitor numbers. In conclusion, the Southwest tourism economy developed through combination of distinctive natural attractions, improved accessibility, cultural heritage resources and policy support, creating employment in services, hospitality and guiding that now significantly contribute to the region's economy.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 7,
        "scheme_points": 13,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
