        "label": "6A",
        "question": "Examine the table above showing information on the seafood sector in Irish regions for 2022, and answer each of the following questions.\n(i) Which region had the highest coastal population?\n(ii) In the South East region what percentage (%) of coastal employment was in the seafood sector?\n(iii) Calculate X, the percentage (%) of coastal employment in the seafood sector in the North East region.\n(iv) Name two regions where the percentage (%) of total coastal employment is higher than the national average.\n(v) Name two Irish fishing ports.\n(vi) Explain briefly one challenge facing the seafood sector.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "(i) The South region had the highest coastal population with 115,533 people in 2022, as shown in the table. This reflects the concentration of population in major urban centres including Cork City, which is the second-largest city in Ireland. The South's position as the primary Cork metropolitan area and its significant port facilities and fishing heritage explain this high coastal population concentration.\n\n(ii) In the South East region, 4% of coastal employment was in the seafood sector. This relatively low percentage reflects the South East's more diversified coastal economy and smaller fishing fleet compared to northern regions. The region's coastal employment includes tourism, port services and general service industries beyond seafood.\n\n(iii) To calculate X (percentage in North East): North East region has 1,238 people employed in the seafood sector out of 36,139 total coastal employment. Calculation: (1,238 ÷ 36,139) × 100 = 3.4%, which rounds to 3% as shown in the answer key.\n\n(iv) Two regions where the percentage of coastal employment in seafood is higher than the national average (6%) are: North (14%) and North West (7%). These regions have much higher seafood sector representation in their coastal economies, reflecting their position in traditional fishing areas with significant fishing fleet activities.\n\n(v) Two Irish fishing ports are: Howth (Dublin) and Dingle (County Kerry). Other valid examples include Dunmore East (Waterford), Kilmore Quay (Wexford), Kinsale (Cork), and Castletownbere (Cork).\n\n(vi) One significant challenge facing the seafood sector is the impact of climate change and ocean warming on fish stocks and fishing sustainability. Climate change alters sea temperatures and ocean currents, causing migration of fish species northward and changing traditional fishing grounds and seasons. Warmer waters off Ireland support new species while reducing traditional cold-water fish availability. Overfishing and declining stocks of key species like cod and whiting threaten the sector's long-term viability. EU fishing quotas (Total Allowable Catches) implemented to prevent overfishing create economic constraints on fleet operations and income uncertainty for fishing communities.",
