    "year": 2025,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "10A",
        "question": "Examine the aerial photograph of Oughterard accompanying this paper. Draw a sketch map of the aerial photograph, half the length and half the breadth. On it, correctly show and label each of the following:\n• A sports field\n• A bridge\n• A church\n• A housing estate in the foreground.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Answer with reference to the aerial photograph of Oughterard issued with this paper.\n\nThe sketch is drawn to half the length and half the breadth of the photograph, with four ruled sides. Because it is an oblique aerial photograph, locate features by dividing the frame into three across — left, centre, right — and three from front to back — foreground, middleground, background.\n\nWhat to show and label:\n• A sports field — outlined as a closed shape and labelled. A sports field is recognised by its regular rectangular shape, its flat grass surface, its marked lines and sometimes goalposts or floodlights.\n• A bridge — marked at a point where a road crosses the river, and labelled.\n• A church — marked in its correct position and labelled. A church is recognised by its steeple or tower, its cruciform shape and the graveyard often beside it.\n• A housing estate in the foreground — outlined as a closed area in the nearest third of the photograph and labelled. Housing estates are recognised by rows of small buildings of similar size and shape, each with a garden, served by estate roads or cul-de-sacs.\n\nMarks: 4 for the sketch outline, then 4 for each of the four features — showing it in approximately the correct position and proportion, and labelling it. The housing estate must be drawn in the foreground to earn its showing marks, and a feature shown but not labelled scores nothing for showing.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 13,
        "scheme_points": 4,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "10B",
        "question": "Examine the effectiveness of urban planning strategies in solving two of the following urban problems, with reference to any urban area(s) that you have studied:\n• Pollution\n• Transport\n• Housing.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "URBAN PLANNING STRATEGIES EFFECTIVENESS EXAMINING TWO PROBLEMS:\n\nPROBLEM 1 - TRANSPORT: Dublin's Bus Rapid Transit (BRT) system exemplifies urban planning addressing transport congestion. The dedicated bus corridors reduce private vehicle traffic by providing fast, reliable public transport, decreasing congestion on main corridors (30% reduction on implemented routes). This strategy effectively addresses transport by prioritizing mass transit over individual vehicles, reducing overall traffic volumes. However, limited network coverage means suburban commuters still rely on private cars, limiting effectiveness across entire metropolitan area. The strategy shows partial effectiveness—highly effective in corridor areas but insufficient for broader metropolitan transport solutions.\n\nPROBLEM 2 - HOUSING: Singapore's public housing program demonstrates planning effectiveness addressing affordability and density. Over 80% of Singapore's population lives in government-built Housing and Development Board apartments, providing affordable housing for massive population on limited land. This strategy effectively solves both affordability and density challenges through centralized planning, but reduces housing diversity and creates socioeconomic segregation. The effectiveness demonstrates that large-scale public housing provision can address housing shortages, though it reflects specific societal priorities and may not suit all cultural contexts.\n\nBRIEF COMPARATIVE ASSESSMENT: Both strategies show effectiveness addressing their specific problems, though neither is universally perfect. Transport solutions require integrated approaches (bus, metro, cycling infrastructure together); housing solutions require balancing affordability, quality, and social diversity. Effectiveness depends on sustained government investment, maintenance, and adaptation to changing needs.\n\nALTERNATIVE PROBLEMS: Pollution—Barcelona's Low Emission Zones reduced vehicle emissions 15% by restricting polluting vehicles; Housing—Vienna's social housing program covers 60% population with high-quality affordable apartments; Transport—Copenhagen's cycling infrastructure reduced car trips 20% through infrastructure investment.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 13,
        "scheme_points": 13,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "10C",
        "question": "Population movements have an impact on donor and receiver regions. Examine one impact of migration on donor regions and one impact of migration on receiver regions, using example(s) that you have studied.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "MIGRATION IMPACTS ON DONOR AND RECEIVER REGIONS:\n\nIMPACT ON DONOR REGIONS: Brain drain represents a significant negative migration impact. When young, educated, skilled workers emigrate seeking higher incomes and better opportunities abroad (e.g., Irish emigration to USA/Australia, Philippine emigration to Middle East), donor regions lose valuable human capital. This reduces innovation capacity, diminishes labor supply in key sectors (healthcare, education, technology), and creates skills shortages making remaining population less competitive. Rural Ireland experienced significant depopulation as educated youth migrated to Dublin, USA, or Australia, leaving aging populations with reduced economic dynamism and service provision. Government investments in education become lost as trained workers generate economic benefits elsewhere. However, remittances provide economic benefit—Filipino migrant workers send home approximately 10% of family income, contributing significantly to national GDP and household incomes despite population loss.\n\nIMPACT ON RECEIVER REGIONS: Dubai/UAE experience demonstrates receiver region benefits but also challenges. Massive immigration (90% of population non-citizen) provided critical labor building infrastructure and servicing economies, generating economic growth and development. However, migration creates social cohesion challenges—cultural diversity strains social integration; employment competition creates tensions between migrant and citizen workers; housing demand drives price inflation; healthcare and education systems face capacity strain. Singapore shows different pattern—controlled migration of skilled workers supports economic growth without generating social unrest, as immigration policy carefully manages numbers and selects high-skilled workers. Receiver regions gain economically but face infrastructure pressures and social integration challenges requiring careful policy management.\n\nBOTH IMPACTS INTERCONNECTED: Global migration represents system where some regions lose working-age population while others gain. Donor regions suffer economically but receive remittances and may eventually attract return migrants with capital/skills. Receiver regions grow but face integration challenges. Most effective migration management requires both regions cooperating on policy.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 13,
        "scheme_points": 12,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
